Despite these advances, most existing studies optimise signal timing
independently of lane allocation, assume static roadway supply, or neglect
spillback-aware corridor-level coordination.  This paper addresses these gaps
through three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item An \textbf{exact clairvoyant MILP formulation} (\cref{sec:milp})
        cast as a time-expanded scheduling problem--intersection settings
        changing per time step--with per-bus timetable-adherence constraints,
        start-variable contiguity, integer queue-waiting delay sums, and
        explicit offset state propagation, shown equivalent to a Markovian
        (Bellman) formulation whose transition guards are the rule-based TSP
        mechanisms.  The MILP is a \emph{proof-of-concept formulation}: it
        defines the control problem and is never solved (\cref{sec:milp_status}).
  \item Eight \textbf{online method families} (\cref{sec:sol_centralised,sec:sol_marl_mp})
        covering tabular Q-learning, Harmony Search, CTM grid search, CTM
        Q-learning, Nash bargaining, MARL, max-pressure, and a centralised
        per-second controller--the deployable \emph{handling mechanism}: each
        solves one greedy single-event subproblem per detection from estimated
        state.  Only the delay-based families inherit the MILP's objective
        (through the shared $Z_1$ commit layer); the others pursue distinct
        objectives (fairness, stability, learned policy, rules) and are
        compared on the common microsimulation criterion, not claimed to
        approximate the MILP.
  \item \textbf{Empirical evaluation} on the Kelvin Grove corridor (Brisbane)
        using Aimsun Next microsimulation, with a three-phase batch design
        (95 champion-search runs, 50 bus-demand runs, 300 demand/intervention
        runs) covering strategy comparison and parameter sweeps.
\end{enumerate}






