Every timing magnitude is an integer at $1\,$s resolution -- signals commit
whole seconds (the controller writes integer durations), so the tokens are
$\texttt{GE}_{\delta}$ with $\delta \in \Z_{\ge 1}$ in the admissible band,
never a coarse $\{10,20,30\}$ preset.  The legacy discrete evaluators listed
fixed candidates ($\texttt{GE}_{5/10/15}$, $\texttt{INS}_{10/15/20}$) purely
as a candidate set; the modern solvers size $\delta$ on the integer lattice
directly (closed-form deficit, DE/rand/1/bin, exhaustive lattice argmax, or
golden-section), and the exact program's duration bound
$\delta_{i,k} \in [\delta_k^{\min}, \delta_k^{\max}]$ is a contiguous integer
range, not a grid.  Variable-phase (VP) and phase-rotation
(PHASE\_ROT) actions extend the action space with phase-sequence control,
described in Sections~\ref{sec:phase_skip} and~\ref{sec:phase_rot}.

\noindent\textbf{GE while the bus phase is red.}  Green extension lengthens whichever phase
is green \emph{now}.  When the bus phase is red, the live green belongs to
conflicting traffic, so extending it pushes the bus green further into the
future and the bus waits longer than under no action.  Every method therefore
checks phase validity first (\cref{eq:online_phase}): GE is admissible
only while the bus phase is live; otherwise the controller considers
insertion or no action.  This check, like the balance guard of
\cref{sec:online_guards}, is an admissibility test applied to a proposed
action rather than a constraint of the exact program.

\subsection{Variable Phase Action and Phase Skipping}
\label{sec:phase_skip}

% TODO(phase-timings-as-actions): extend the action space beyond the
% bus-phase-centric GE/INS/ER/GR set to full PHASE TIMING actions -- each
% phase's green duration as a continuous decision variable, re-splitting
% green across the whole cycle (subject to cycle conservation
% sum(g_p) = C - L and min-green bounds). The candidate set then becomes
% phase-timing plans rather than single-phase adjustments, and the Pareto
% front of \cref{sec:selection_layer} is built over those plans.
% Requires per-phase D/D/1 delay estimates (already available from the
% GE reward kinematics) evaluated per re-split plan.

The standard action set \{\texttt{NO\_ACTION, GE, GR, INS, ER}\} does not
allow the controller to alter the \emph{sequence} of phases within a cycle.
Three related terms appear below and mean different things.
The Variable Phase (VP) \emph{mechanism} of Long et al.
\citep{Long2022PhaseSkipping} adds a phase-selection action: at each decision
step, the controller chooses the \emph{next} phase index from $\Lambda_i =
\{0, 1, \dots, |\mathcal{P}_i|-1\}$.
\emph{Phase skipping} is the resulting \emph{effect}: a phase is served zero
green because it was not selected next, subject to the service guards below.
\emph{Phase rotation} (Sec.~\ref{sec:phase_rot}) is the cycle-level version:
reordering the whole sequence once per cycle rather than choosing the next
phase step by step.

\paragraph{Formal definition.}
At intersection $i$ and step $t$, the VP action selects:
\begin{equation}
  a_{i,t}^{\mathrm{VP}} \in \Lambda_i
    = \{0, 1, \dots, |\mathcal{P}_i|-1\}
  \label{eq:vp_action}
\end{equation}
The controller skips phase $p$ if $p$ is not selected as the next served phase,
advancing directly to the chosen phase after the current intergreen.

\paragraph{Phase skipping guards (invalid action masking).}
Not all elements of $\Lambda_i$ are always worth selecting.  Two service
guards from \citep{Long2022PhaseSkipping} restrict the selectable set.  These
are bus-priority policy, not safety constraints: genuine safety (minimum
greens, intergreen clearance, pedestrian times) is enforced separately by the
capacity-legality bounds R7.  What follows decides when skipping is
\emph{worthwhile}, never when it is \emph{safe}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Queue threshold:} Phase $p$ is skippable at $t$ only when its
        queue does not exceed the maximum skip-queue length
        $Q_{\max}^{\mathrm{skip}}$:
        \begin{equation}
          p \text{ skippable at } t
          \;\Longleftrightarrow\;
          L_{Q,p}(t) \le Q_{\max}^{\mathrm{skip}}.
          \label{eq:skip_queue}
        \end{equation}
        This prevents starvation of heavily loaded phases.
  \item \textbf{Consecutive-skip rule:} Phase $p$ is skippable at $t$ only if it
        was not itself skipped at $t-1$ while vehicles are detected on its
        approach:
        \begin{equation}
          \bigl(a_{i,t-1}^{\mathrm{VP}} \ne p \;\text{ or }\; n_{i,p}(t) = 0\bigr)
          \;\Longrightarrow\;
          p \text{ skippable at } t,
          \label{eq:skip_consec}
        \end{equation}
        where $n_{i,p}(t)$ is the detected vehicle count on phase $p$'s approach.
\end{enumerate}
The set of valid next-phase indices at step $t$--the \emph{invalid action
mask}--is:
\begin{equation}
  \Lambda_i^*(t) = \bigl\{p \in \Lambda_i :
    \text{Eq.\,\ref{eq:skip_queue} and Eq.\,\ref{eq:skip_consec} hold for all }
    p' \ne p \bigr\}
  \label{eq:iam}
\end{equation}
The controller selects $a_{i,t}^{\mathrm{VP}} \in \Lambda_i^*(t)$ only, so
masked actions are never evaluated or executed.  This invalid action masking
(IAM) \citep{Long2022PhaseSkipping} is essential for starvation-free
phase-sequence control: without it, a learning controller could repeatedly
skip a high-demand phase, causing queue spillback.

\subsection{Phase Rotation}
\label{sec:phase_rot}

Phase rotation \citep{Yu2022PhaseRotation} jointly optimises the phase
\emph{sequence}, cycle length, and green splits, rather than accepting the
baseline phase order as fixed.  A set $\mathcal{R}_i$ of candidate sequences
is enumerated offline; at each cycle $T$, the controller selects one:

\paragraph{Binary sequence selection.}
At intersection $i$ and cycle $T$, let $z_{i,r}^T \in \{0,1\}$ indicate
whether candidate sequence $r \in \mathcal{R}_i$ is implemented:
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{r \in \mathcal{R}_i} z_{i,r}^T = 1
  \qquad \forall\,i \in \mathcal{I},\; T
  \label{eq:rot_select}
\end{equation}
Exactly one sequence is active per cycle.  For a standard four-phase
intersection, $|\mathcal{R}_i| = 3$ distinct cyclic orderings are typically
sufficient; a six-phase intersection admits up to $|\mathcal{R}_i| = 8$
candidate sequences \citep{Yu2022PhaseRotation}.

\paragraph{Joint green-split optimisation.}
For each candidate sequence $r \in \mathcal{R}_i$ the green times
$g_{i,p,T}$ follow a Webster-type allocation \citep{Webster1958Traffic}.
Read \cref{eq:rot_green} as the closed-form allocation rule for generating
candidates---not as another exact-program objective: it chooses the split
that makes the uniform-delay proxy as small as the cycle budget and the
minimum greens allow. In that equation $q_{i,p}$ is the measured arrival
rate on phase $p$, $C_i$ the cycle length, $\mu_{i,p}$ the saturation
discharge rate, and $L_i$ the total lost time. Ignoring the minimum-green
bounds the rule has a closed form:
\begin{equation}
  \min_{g_{i,p,T}} \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}_i}
    \frac{\bigl(q_{i,p}\,C_i\bigr)^2}{2\,\mu_{i,p}\,g_{i,p,T}}
  \quad \text{subject to} \quad
  \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}_i} g_{i,p,T} = C_i - L_i,\quad
  g_{i,p,T} \ge G_i^{\min}
  \label{eq:rot_green}
\end{equation}
Ignoring the minimum-green bounds this has a closed form: with multiplier
$\lambda$ on the budget, $-a_p/g_{i,p,T}^2 + \lambda = 0$ gives green in
proportion to $\sqrt{a_p}$ with
$a_p = (q_{i,p} C_i)^2/(2\mu_{i,p})$, i.e.\ $g_{i,p,T} \propto
q_{i,p}/\sqrt{\mu_{i,p}}$, rescaled to the budget; bounds are enforced by
clipping at $G_i^{\min}$ and re-normalising the remainder.  The sequence with
the lowest predicted cycle delay is then selected (exactly one active
sequence per cycle by Eq.\,\ref{eq:rot_select}).

\paragraph{Rotation benefit threshold and coordination.}
Let $\Delta Z_1(r)$ be the predicted passenger-delay \emph{saving} of sequence
$r$ relative to the fixed-time baseline over one cycle, in seconds
(positive means better). It is not measured after the fact: each candidate is
scored by the controller's own one-cycle predictor, which propagates the
candidate's gate profile through the queue recursion
(\cref{eq:timing_flow_chain}) on the same person-delay currency $Z_1$
(\cref{eq:Z1}) used everywhere else, so a sequence's edge is a predicted
quantity of the same units as the objective. $\Theta_{\mathrm{rot}}$ is a
controller-set hysteresis threshold fixed before the run, not a quantity the
method estimates: it states how much predicted passenger delay a reorder must
save before the offset disruption is worth making. Rotation commits to the
best candidate $r^*$ only if its edge over keeping the current sequence
$r_0$ clears that threshold:
\begin{equation}
  \Delta Z_1(r^*) - \Delta Z_1(r_0) \;\ge\; \Theta_{\mathrm{rot}}
  \label{eq:rot_threshold}
\end{equation}
Below this threshold the existing sequence is retained.  Each intersection
solves its own small problem independently once per cycle (at most 8
sequences, each a closed-form split, so microseconds), yet the outcome stays
coordinated for two reasons.  First, every candidate's predicted delay
includes the corridor offset and progression coupling, so a sequence that
breaks the green wave prices that damage inside $\Delta Z_1(r)$ and loses on
merit.  Second, the threshold vetoes marginal reorderings whose small local
gain is not worth the offset disruption they cause.  Coordination therefore
enters through shared offset state and corridor-priced costs rather than a
joint multi-intersection optimiser.

\subsection{Key Notation}
The recurring symbols of the controller layer are fixed once here; each is a
definition, not a constraint:
\begin{align*}
  d_b^{\text{NA}}  &\triangleq \text{estimated bus delay under NO\_ACTION (s)} \\
  d_b^{\delta}     &\triangleq \text{bus delay after action } \delta \text{ (s)} \\
  n_b              &\triangleq \text{bus occupancy (pax)} \\
  \Delta_{\text{car}}(\delta) &\triangleq \text{additional cross-traffic pax\,s caused by } \delta \\
  \bar{\tau}_{b,i} &\triangleq \text{scheduled (timetable) arrival of bus } b \text{ at } i \text{ (s)} \\
  \sigma_{b,i}     &\triangleq \text{schedule deviation } \tau_{b,i} - \bar{\tau}_{b,i} \text{ (steps, $+$ = late)} \\
  \sigma_{\text{in}}, \sigma_{\text{out}} &\triangleq \text{headway deviation before/after action (s)} \\
  \tau_{\text{late}} &\triangleq \text{lateness threshold, default 30\,s}
\end{align*}

