\paragraph{The decision, in one line.} The launch plan fixes only the initial
condition: \emph{when} the plan says each gate should be open, with the cycle,
green, and offset bounds of the timing family. The decision variables of the
program are the time-indexed open/closed indicators
$x_{i,p,t}$ together with the actions that move them
(\cref{eq:x_primary,eq:plan_gate}). Nothing in the objective is chosen---only
the gate/action trajectory is---so every reported quantity is a consequence of
that trajectory rather than an input to it.

\paragraph{Integer time.} Time is integer-indexed throughout this section.
The step is fixed to $\Delta t = 1$\,s and the horizon is
$\Tsp = \{0,1,\dots,T\} \subset \Z_{\ge 0}$; every duration, green, offset,
travel time, and arrival clock is an integer number of steps (seconds).
This matches the implementation, which commits all durations at integer-second
resolution: each action's duration is an integer variable bounded between
$\delta_k^{\min}$ and $\delta_k^{\max}$ (for instance GE in $5$--$15$ steps and
INS in $10$--$20$ steps), so it is the \emph{bounds} that are fixed data, not a
fixed list of admissible values --- the program is free to choose any integer in
the interval.  No continuous time variable appears in the exact program.

\paragraph{What is solved here, and what is not.} This program answers one
question: if every arrival, queue, and bus timetable were known in advance
for the whole horizon, which signal should show green at every intersection
at every second so that total passenger delay is minimised and every bus
meets its timetable?  The inputs are the street state second by second:
which signal aspect is active, how long each queue is and how fast it
discharges, where each intersection sits in its cycle relative to its
neighbours, where each bus is against its timetable, and how passenger delay
accumulates.  The outputs are the green or not-green choice for every phase
at every second, plus the record of when each bus arrives against its
timetable.  The program ends there.  The traditional TSP rules R1--R7 plus the
controller rules R8--R11 are \emph{not} constraints of this program; they are
part of the solution algorithm (\cref{sec:rule_tsp}), because each is a
test of whether an action is admissible \emph{at this moment}, which a
whole-horizon program never evaluates.  Nothing here estimates, learns,
or searches.  Everything that touches live sensors (phase and spillback
readings, estimated arrival times, veto ratios, congestion fractions,
Q-values, Harmony Search improvisation, Nash utilities, measured feeds, and
the net-benefit and $Z_4$ selection logic) belongs to the deployed
controller in \cref{sec:solutions,sec:selection_layer}:
those are the estimated-state implementations of the same rules, and none
of them appears as a constraint of this program.  The deployed code never
solves this program as a whole; at each bus detection it solves one small
greedy problem for one junction and one action (the delay-based learners
approximate a single Bellman stage, while Nash bargaining, max-pressure,
and the centralised rules pursue their own objectives under the same guard
protocol; see \cref{tab:methods}).
A map of every variable and constraint, stating what is given,
what is chosen, and what is returned, is \cref{sec:appendix_program}.

\subsection{What the Program Computes, Second by Second}
\label{sec:sched_interp}

The program is best read as a second-by-second timetable over the network.
Time is the integer grid $\Tsp = \{0,1,\dots,T\}$ with
$\Delta t = 1$\,s.  What changes per time step is the \emph{operation}: for
every intersection $i$ and every integer step $t$ the schedule specifies
which gate is open, $x_{i,p,t} \in \{0,1\}$ (at most one phase open; none
open during intergreen clearance), with realised greens
$g_{i,p,t} = x_{i,p,t}$ following from \cref{eq:gate_green}.  TSP
\emph{actions} (GE/PI/ER/GR/NA with integer durations $\delta_{i,k}$) do not
replace the operation; they \emph{constrain} it around detection events via
the action-to-gate coupling of
\cref{eq:couple_ge,eq:couple_gr2}: a Green Extension forces
additional bus-gate-open steps, a Phase Insertion forces inserted bus steps,
an Early Red forces early closure of a conflicting gate, and a Green
Reallocation moves open steps from a donor gate to the bus gate within the
same cycle.  The optimizer therefore chooses \emph{when} each operation
switches, \emph{which} action forces the switch, \emph{how long} the forced
opening lasts (integer seconds), and \emph{how} each change propagates to
neighbouring intersections through modulo-cycle offsets.
Each row below states one piece of that timetable in words and points to
where the program computes it:

