Three properties keep this exact layer self-contained:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Parameter closure.}  Every coefficient--arrival counts,
        initial queues, saturation rates, integer hyperplane constants,
        free-flow lags, timetable--is a parameter fixed before the solve.
        The physical models of \cref{sec:dynamics} enter only through
        these pre-computed integers; no estimation or statistical equation is
        solved inside the program.
  \item \textbf{No online machinery, and no policy rules.}  The program contains
        neither. There is no detector, no clock, and no test of the corridor's
        current state, so there is nothing for an online mechanism to gate: the
        traditional TSP philosophy is not written here as clairvoyant policy
        constraints but supplied by the solution algorithm
        (\cref{sec:rule_tsp}), which applies balance, wrong-phase
        rejection, cooldown, spillback protection, detection horizon, takeover,
        and capacity legality to each candidate action at the instant it is
        proposed. What appears exclusively in
        \cref{sec:solutions}--\ref{sec:selection_layer}
        is that machinery together with its estimates: live sensors
        ($\hat{\alpha}, \hat{\nu}^{\text{spill}}$, measured queues),
        estimated ETAs, veto ratios, congestion fractions, learning updates,
        reward functions, bargaining utilities, search heuristics, measured
        feeds, and the net-benefit/$Z_4$ selection logic.  None of the online
        machinery modifies the feasible set $\Ucal$ or objectives defined here.
  \item \textbf{Integer exactness, not tractability.}  All time quantities are
        integers ($\Delta t = 1$\,s).  Solving the program to optimality over
        the full horizon is computationally prohibitive; the delay-based methods of
        \cref{sec:solutions} are therefore statistical surrogates that
        approximate--never replicate--its decisions under real-time
        constraints, solving one single-event greedy subproblem per detection
        from an estimated (not clairvoyant) state.  Nash bargaining,
        max-pressure, and the centralised controller do not approximate the
        MILP's decisions: they optimise their own objectives (fairness,
        stability/throughput, rule-based response) inside the same guard
        protocol and are judged on the same microsimulation criterion.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Equivalent Markovian Formulation: the MIP as a Solved Markov Process}
\label{sec:markovian}

The time-expanded schedule of \cref{sec:sched_interp} can equally be
read as the solution of a \emph{finite-horizon Markovian decision process}:
at every step the controller observes a state, picks an admissible action,
and the corridor transitions deterministically.  Because every transition in
the program is a deterministic (linear or linearised) function, deciding the
whole schedule in advance and deciding it stage-by-stage from the current
state produce the same optimal actions--the MIP is the flattened Bellman
recursion.

\paragraph{State, decision, transition.}
The Markov state at integer step $t$ collects only plant quantities--no
sensor flags, no heuristic thresholds:
\begin{equation}
  X_t = \bigl(
    \{\,x_{i,p,t},\, e_{i,p,t},\, c_{i,t},\, Q_{i,m,t},\,
      \phi_{i,t},\,\pi^{\text{POG}}_{i,t}\,\}_{i,\, p,\, m},\;
    \{\,\tau_{b,i},\, \sigma_{b,i}\,\}_{b \in \Bsp,\, i \in \Rcal_b}
  \bigr)
  \label{eq:markov_state}
\end{equation}
i.e.\ the open-gate operations and realised greens with modulo-cycle offsets,
the predicted queues, the integer Purdue POG fractions, and the integer bus clocks with schedule deviations
(when $\Bsp = \emptyset$ the bus block is empty and $X_t$ is bus-free by construction).
Three points on what the state is and is not.  First, it is per \emph{actual phase}:
$x_{i,p,t}$ is indexed over the full $p\in\Psp_i$, and the ``current phase'' is
simply $\arg\max_p x_{i,p,t}$ (clearance if the argmax is empty)---nothing is
collapsed to main/side.  The main/side split enters only downstream, in delay
attribution by approach class $\sigma(\cdot)$ and in the main-movement reading
$x^{\text{main}}_{i,t}$ of \cref{eq:x_main_reading}; no timing constraint
ever sees a collapsed state.  Second, queues are per \emph{movement and vehicle
class}, $Q_{i,m,t}$ with $m$ ranging over approach$\times$lane-group$\times$turn
$\times$ vehicle class: the bus queue and the car queue on the same approach
are distinct state entries, because the objective prices them at
$\rho_{\text{bus}}=40$ versus $\rho_{\text{car}}=1.5$ and no aggregate can be
split back into what the objective reads.  The phase queue is the held sum
\begin{equation}
  Q_{i,p,t} \;=\; \sum_{m:\,\Psp_i(m)=p} Q_{i,m,t}
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp,
  \label{eq:q_phase_sum}
\end{equation}
not primitive state (initial conditions must satisfy it at $t=0$).
(Realised green $g_{i,p,t}$ is likewise held, $g=x$ by
\cref{eq:gate_green}, and is omitted from $X_t$.)  Consistency between the
two recursions is then exact, not assumed: with lane-additive saturation
$\mu_{i,p}=\sum_{m:p(m)=p}\mu_{i,m}$, arrival aggregation
$A_{i,p,t}=\sum_m A_{i,m,t}$, \cref{eq:q_phase_sum}, and discharge maximal
($\star$) so $d_{i,p,t}=\min(\mu_{i,p}x_{i,p,t},\,Q_{i,p,t}+A_{i,p,t})$, summing
\cref{eq:mv_q} over $m:p(m)=p$ reproduces \cref{eq:gate_queue} term for
term whenever the approach is not starved ($Q_{i,p,t}+A_{i,p,t}\ge
\mu_{i,p}x_{i,p,t}$); under starvation both sides agree at the
presence-capped rate, never negative, never double-counted.  Third, the switch indicators $w^{\text{on/off}}$ are \emph{not} in
the state, but their two clocks are: elapsed green $e_{i,p,t}\in\Z_{\ge 0}$
($0$ if shut), with $e_{i,p,t+1}=(e_{i,p,t}+1)x_{i,p,t+1}$, fixes
$w^{\text{on}}_{i,p,t}=\mathbf{1}\{e_{i,p,t}=1\}$; and clearance elapsed
$c_{i,t}\in\Z_{\ge 0}$ ($0$ while any gate is open, else $+1$ per shut step),
with $c_{i,t+1}=(c_{i,t}+1)(1-\sum_px_{i,p,t+1})$, fixes $w^{\text{off}}$ and
restates \cref{eq:gate_clear} markovially (a conflicting gate opens only at
$c_{i,t}\ge I_{\text{ig}}$).  Without $e$ the dwell clock is history-dependent;
without $c$ the clearance clock is too; with both, every switch indicator is
fixed by present state alone.  This matches the deployed controller's own state
(``current phase, elapsed time'' per intersection, plus its clearance timer),
so the exact and online programs observe the same signal.
(Notation: $e_{i,p,t}$ is elapsed green on phase $p$; $e_{b,i,t}$ is the bus-$b$
arrival indicator at $i$ --- different objects, distinguished by subscript.)
Crucially, $X_t$ is the \emph{full corridor vector}: the decision at upstream
intersection $i$ at step $t$ observes the queue entries $Q_{j,p,t}$ for
\emph{every} downstream intersection $j$, including $j \ne i$, at the
\emph{same} integer step $t$.  There is no local-only state; $u_{i,t} =
\pi_i(X_t)$ is a corridor-state feedback law.  The control at step $t$ is the
event slice $u_t = \{y_{i,k,t}\}_{i,k}$ (with starts $\{s_{i,k,t}\}_{i,k}$
held as its rising edge by \cref{eq:start_def,eq:start_active});
the operation $\{x_{i,p,t}\}_{i,p}$ stored in $X_t$ is the realisation of
$u_t$ through the action-to-gate coupling
(\cref{eq:couple_ge,eq:couple_gr2}), so $y$ and $x$ are never
chosen independently.  All rule auxiliaries below
($\Psi_{i,t},\Lambda_{i,t},\zeta_{ij,t},\chi_{b,i},\epsilon^{\pm}_{ij,t},
e_{b,i,t},w^{\text{on/off}}_{i,p,t},d_{i,p,t},D_{i,s,v,t},
\delta^{\text{eff}}_{i,s,t},z_{i,t},\pi^{\text{POG}}_{i,t},L^{\text{prog}}_{i,t},o^{\text{shift}}_{i,t},
e^{\text{trig}}_{i,t},b^{\text{present}}_{i,t},h_{i,t},o^{\text{empty}}_{i,p,t}$) are deterministic \emph{outputs}
held by $(X_t,u_t)$--fixed by equalities/inequalities in their defining
subsections--never independent inputs, so no constraint floats disconnected
from $X_t$ (see Holding Lemma, Sec.~\ref{sec:holding}).  A decision
$u_t$ selects the operation in effect at $t$ (equivalently,
which action starts at $t$), and the plant evolves through the per-step
recursions already defined in this section:
\begin{equation}
  X_{t+1} = F(X_t, u_t)
  \label{eq:markov_trans}
\end{equation}
where $F$ bundles the signal-change-indicator and queue recursions
(\cref{eq:gate_switch,eq:gate_queue}), the elapsed-green and
clearance recursions ($e_{i,p,t+1}=(e_{i,p,t}+1)x_{i,p,t+1}$,
$c_{i,t+1}=(c_{i,t}+1)(1-\sum_px_{i,p,t+1})$), the movement-queue recursion
(\cref{eq:mv_q}) with the phase-queue held sum, the modulo-cycle offset
recursion (\cref{eq:offset_state}), the Purdue POG recursion
(\cref{eq:pog_ewma,eq:pog_shift}), the integer bus-arrival propagation
(\cref{eq:arr_prop}, vacuous when $\Bsp = \emptyset$), the single realised-green definition
(\cref{eq:gate_green}) as constrained by the action-to-gate coupling
(\cref{eq:couple_ge,eq:couple_gr2}), and the queue-waiting delay
bounds (\cref{eq:mv_q,eq:mv_waitsum}).  $X_{t+1}$ depends on
$(X_t, u_t)$ only--the
Markov property holds by construction, which is precisely why the
time-expanded grid needs no additional history variables.

\paragraph{One layer only: physics.} The exact admissible set is physical
feasibility, $\Ucal(X_t)$: at most one
open gate per $i,t$ modulo intergreen/min-green
(\cref{eq:gate_excl,eq:gate_dwell}), green/cycle bounds
(\cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds}), and finite integer
queues/discharges.  There is no second, rule-restricted admissible set
$\Ucal_{\text{rule}}$: the physics-only optimum is the program's optimum, and
it shows what the street allows. The conditional-priority philosophy of
\cref{sec:rule_tsp} narrows that set only at decision time, on estimated
state, so its optimum is a property of the solution algorithm rather than of
this program.  Neither layer contains sensors or search: congestion/viability
gates, cost vetoes, measured-side floors, cascade pricing, Q-values, Harmony
improvisation, and Nash utilities all act on an \emph{estimated} state
$\hat{X}_t$ (unreliable ETAs, measured queues, live phases); they are the
real-time safety checks defined
in \cref{sec:online_guards} as $\hat{u}_t \in \hat{\Ucal}(\hat{X}_t)$
and Appendix~A0 executes them.  The deployed controller may therefore block a
grant that this program would deem feasible---that gap is the price
of real-time sensing and greedy single-event decisions, not a model
infeasibility.  Capacity legality
(\cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds}) is the one check the program and the
online layer share.

\paragraph{Bellman recursion and exact equivalence.}
The stage cost is the \emph{predicted} delay incurred in step $t$--the
passenger-weighted area under the predicted queues plus the schedule
deviation of every bus (zero buses = zero bus terms). The Purdue
displaced-platoon term is deliberately absent: it is priced from estimated
state and belongs to the online methods (Sec.~\ref{sec:objectives}), not to
this exact program:
\begin{equation}
  c_t(X,u) \;=\; \sum_{i \in \Isp} \sum_{p \in \Psp_i}
    \rho_{v(p)}\, Q_{i,p,t}(X,u)
    \;+\; \sum_{b \in \Bsp} \omega_b\, \sigma^{+}_{b,i(t)}
  \label{eq:stage_cost}
\end{equation}
so the program's objective is exactly the total delay under prediction
accumulated by the recursion ($\Delta t = 1$, so one step of queue $Q$
contributes $Q$ vehicle-seconds).  The optimal value function is defined by the
Bellman optimality equation
\begin{equation}
  V_t(X) = \min_{u \in \Ucal(X)}
    \Bigl[\, c_t(X, u) + V_{t+1}\!\bigl(F(X, u)\bigr) \Bigr],
  \qquad V_{T+1} \equiv 0
  \label{eq:bellman}
\end{equation}
where $\Ucal(X)$ is physical feasibility only (at most one open gate,
intergreen/min-green, green/cycle bounds).  Unrolling
\cref{eq:bellman} over the horizon reproduces the time-expanded MIP
exactly: the binary start variables $s_{i,k,t}$ enumerate the minimisation
over $u_t$, the physical-system constraints implement $\Ucal(X_t)$, the
transition equations implement $F$, and the additive objective implements
the sum of stage costs.  Hence
\begin{center}
\emph{solving the MIP $\;\equiv\;$ solving the Markovian process to
optimality with perfect state information;}
\end{center}
there is no optimality gap between the two views.  The practical difference
is computational and informational: the recursion of \cref{eq:bellman} is
intractable online and assumes the clairvoyant $F$ and true $X_t$, whereas the
methods of \cref{sec:solutions} greedily minimise an estimated stage
cost from an estimated state $\hat{X}_t$ subject to the strictly tighter
online guard set $\hat{\Ucal}(\hat{X}_t)$ of
\cref{sec:online_guards}--estimation plus additional blocking, never
additional feasibility.

\begin{theorem}[MILP--Bellman equivalence]
\label{thm:milp_bellman}
Let $x_{t+1} = F(x_t,u_t)$ be the deterministic transition defined by the
queue recursion (\cref{eq:gate_queue}), the offset recursion
(\cref{eq:offset_state}), the Purdue POG recursion
(\cref{eq:pog_ewma,eq:pog_shift}), the signal-change-indicator logic
(\cref{eq:gate_switch,eq:gate_dwell}), the bus-arrival propagation
(\cref{eq:arr_prop}, vacuous when $\Bsp = \emptyset$), the realised-green definition
(\cref{eq:gate_green}) under the action-to-gate coupling
(\cref{eq:couple_ge,eq:couple_gr2}), and the gate logic, with
stage cost $c_t(x_t,u_t)$ equal to the passenger-delay term of
\cref{eq:stage_cost}.  Then the finite-horizon single-episode deterministic problem
(one contiguous block per $(i,k)$; repeated grants are receding-horizon
re-solves per \cref{eq:reopt})
$\min_{u_0,\dots,u_T}\sum_{t=0}^{T}c_t(x_t,u_t)$ subject to the plant
constraints has the same optimal value and the same optimal solution set as
the MILP of \cref{eq:start_def,eq:sched_cap} plus Purdue state
(\cref{eq:pog_ewma,eq:pog_loss_zero}) and trigger state
(\cref{eq:bus_present,eq:trig_source}) with criterion
    \cref{eq:Z1,eq:opt_crit} (plant-only form): $V_0(x_0) = Z^*$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\emph{Step 1: state and Markov property.} Every MILP variable that
propagates through time is contained in $X_t$ of \cref{eq:markov_state}:
queues $Q_{i,p,t}$, bus clocks $\tau_{b,i}$ and deviations $\sigma_{b,i}$
(empty when $\Bsp = \emptyset$),
offsets $\phi_{i,t}$, POG fractions $\pi^{\text{POG}}_{i,t}$, and gate operations $x_{i,p,t}$/$g_{i,p,t}$.  The
update of each block uses only $(X_t,u_t)$ with $u_t = \{y_{i,k,t}\}_{i,k}$
(the event slice; $x_{i,p,t}$ is its realisation via coupling), so
$\Pr(x_{t+1}\mid x_t,u_t)=\Pr(x_{t+1}\mid x_0,\dots,x_t,u_0,\dots,u_t)$:
the process is Markov by construction and needs no history variables.

\emph{Step 2: determinism.} All transitions listed above are deterministic
(linear or linearised) functions: given $(X_t,u_t)$ there is exactly one
$X_{t+1}=F(X_t,u_t)$.  No expectation appears.

\emph{Step 3: control correspondence (bijection, both directions).} Scope is
single-episode: one contiguous block per $(i,k)$ over the horizon, enforced by
\cref{eq:start_once} (repeated grants in deployment correspond to
re-solving this program per detection from the measured state, i.e.\ the
receding-horizon recourse of \cref{eq:reopt}--the online handling
mechanism).  Define $\Phi$ from single-episode control sequences to
schedules: given $(u_0,\dots,u_T)$ with $u_t=\{y_{i,k,t}\}_{i,k}$ satisfying
one-action-per-$(i,t)$ (\cref{eq:excl}) and at most one $0\!\to\!1$ rising
edge per $(i,k)$, set $s_{i,k,t}=y_{i,k,t}(1-y_{i,k,t-1})$ (with
$s_{i,k,0}=y_{i,k,0}$), $\bar{y}_{i,k}=\sum_t s_{i,k,t}$,
$\delta_{i,k}=\sum_t y_{i,k,t}$, and force $x_{i,p,t}$ through
\cref{eq:couple_ge,eq:couple_gr2}; all downstream state
$(Q,\phi,\tau,\sigma)$ follows deterministically from Step~2.  Define $\Psi$
from schedules to controls by projection: given a feasible
$(y,s,x,\dots)$, drop everything but $u_t=\{y_{i,k,t}\}_{i,k}$, which
satisfies the per-step admissible set by
\cref{eq:gate_excl,eq:gate_dwell} and \ref{eq:g_bounds}--\ref{eq:c_bounds}.
$\Psi(\Phi(u))=u$ holds because $\Phi$ copies $y$ verbatim; $\Phi(\Psi(S))=S$
holds because, given $y$, the starts are the unique minimal feasible rising
edges (Holding Lemma, Sec.~\ref{sec:holding}), $x$ is forced by the coupling,
and the remaining state is a deterministic function of $(X_0,u)$.  Hence
single-episode control sequences and feasible MILP schedules are in
one-to-one correspondence.  This is the key bijection.

\emph{Step 4: objective equivalence.} The MILP objective is
$Z=\sum_t c_t(X_t,u_t)$ where $c_t$ is implemented by the delay variables
and queue-waiting bounds (\cref{eq:mv_q,eq:mv_waitsum}).  The Bellman recursion is
$V_t(X)=\min_{u}[c_t(X,u)+V_{t+1}(F(X,u))]$, $V_{T+1}\equiv0$, whose repeated
substitution gives $V_0(x_0)=\min_{u_0,\dots,u_T}\sum_t c_t(X_t,u_t)$:
exactly the MILP objective over the bijected feasible sets.

\emph{Step 5: conclusion.} Feasible schedules and feasible policies coincide,
both optimise the identical cumulative cost under identical transitions and
constraints, so the optimal solution sets coincide and $Z^*=V_0(x_0)$. \qed
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Deterministic equivalent program]
The MILP is the deterministic equivalent program (DEP) of the
finite-horizon MDP defined by state $X_t$, action $u_t$, transition $F$,
and stage cost $c_t$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every admissible policy induces exactly one feasible trajectory and every
feasible trajectory corresponds to one admissible policy (Theorem~\ref{thm:milp_bellman},
Step~3); hence optimisation over policies equals optimisation over
schedules.  ``Flattened Bellman recursion'' used elsewhere in this paper
means exactly this DEP relationship. \qed
\end{proof}

