\subsubsection{Cycle, Green, and Offsets as Constraints of the One Program}
\label{sec:stage_a}

There is no separate design program and no fixed-time plan standing before the
optimiser.  The cycle, the per-phase green, and the offset are \emph{decisions
of this one program}, carried over time: at every intersection $i$ and cycle
window $c$ the program chooses how much green each phase receives and where the
cycle clock sits, and it may move those quantities step by step through the
actions.  What the timing must satisfy is stated here once; the per-step
constraints that enforce it are the green/cycle bounds of
\cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds} and the offset band of
\cref{eq:offset_band_lin}.  A conventional fixed-time plan is the
\emph{special case} in which the program takes no action and the bounds pin the
timing to a single point---a warm start, not a prior stage.

\paragraph{Timing inputs.} For each intersection $i$ and movement
$m \in \Msp_i$, the arrival flow $A_{i,m}$ (veh/h, integer scaled), and for each
phase $p$ its saturation $\mu_{i,p}$ (\cref{eq:mu_lanes}); for the bus phase
$p_b^i$ the timetable intensity $\vartheta_i$ (buses per cycle) and the
occupancy $\rho_{\text{bus}}$.  These size the bounds.  They are data: the
arrival \emph{count} is absorbed by the queue recursion (\cref{eq:mv_q}),
never by re-optimising the timing against the mean (\cref{sec:stochastic}).

\paragraph{Cycle and its bounds.} The cycle $C_i$ is confined to the integer
bounds $[C_i^{\min}, C_i^{\max}]$ fixed for the junction.  A starting value,
used only to initialise the clock and as a warm start, is the Webster value on
the \emph{critical} flow ratio---the largest ratio among movements that cannot
be served together, so movements sharing an origin section form one independent
group and $Y_i$ is the sum of the group maxima:
\begin{equation}
  Y_i \;=\; \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}_i} \max_{m \in c} \frac{A_{i,m}}{\mu_{i,m}},
  \qquad
  C_i^{0} \;=\; \frac{1.5\,L_i + 5}{1 - Y_i},
  \qquad
  C_i^{\min} \le C_i \le C_i^{\max},
  \label{eq:webster_cycle}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{C}_i$ is the partition of $\Msp_i$ into independent
(concurrent) groups and $L_i$ is lost time.  The clamp to
$[C_i^{\min}, C_i^{\max}]$ is the \emph{constraint}; $C_i^{0}$ is only the
initial point, and when $Y_i \ge 1$ the junction is oversaturated and the
program falls back to the measured queue state.  Integer arithmetic throughout,
so the cycle is a whole number of seconds.

\paragraph{Green splits and the cycle identity.} Green is apportioned to
critical flow, with the bus phase transit-weighted by timetable intensity,
subject to the minimum green and to the exact cycle identity that ties the
per-phase greens, the lost time, and the cycle together:
\begin{align}
  g_{i,p} &\;=\; \max\Bigl( G_i^{\min},\;
      \frac{(C_i - L_i)\,\omega_{i,p}}{\sum_{p'} \omega_{i,p'}} \Bigr),
  \qquad \omega_{i,p} = \frac{A_{i,p}}{\mu_{i,p}}\bigl(1 + \lambda\,\mathbf{1}[p = p_b^i]\vartheta_i\bigr)
    \label{eq:webster_split} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} g_{i,p} + L_i &\;=\; C_i
    \label{eq:split_identity}
\end{align}
with $\lambda$ the fixed transit weight.  These two relations are the
\emph{warm-start/consistency} form of the split; the binding constraints are
the per-window bounds
$\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} \in [G_i^{\min}, G_i^{\max}]$
(\cref{eq:g_bounds}) and the cycle-sum conservation (\cref{eq:c_bounds}), which
hold for every window $c$ and let the program move green between phases (GR)
within the sum.  A split that cannot satisfy the identity, or the minima
$\sum_p G_i^{\min} + L_i \le C_i$, is rejected rather than emitted---the
applied plan never disagrees with its own declared cycle.

\paragraph{Offsets.} Offsets are the modulo cycle-clock positions
$\phi_{i,t}$ of \cref{eq:offset_state}: they are \emph{decisions}, advanced by
the actions and bounded by the inter-intersection band of
\cref{eq:offset_band_lin}.  A starting value follows from progression---the
timetable fixes a desired running time $\Theta^{\text{ff}}_{ij}$ between
consecutive intersections, so the initial offsets $\phi_i^{\text{init}}$ are
placed to support it---but the running value is not fixed:
\begin{equation}
  \bar{\phi}_j \;=\; \Bigl(\bar{\phi}_i + \Theta^{\text{ff}}_{ij}\Bigr) \bmod C,
  \qquad C \in \{C_i, C_j\},
  \label{eq:stage_a_offset}
\end{equation}
the classic two-band progression offset anchored at one corridor reference
intersection.  The program chooses $\phi_{i,t}$ against this anchor under the
band constraint; the online controller of \cref{sec:online_guards} is the
tractable restriction in which the offset is corrected only when the realised
overlap approaches the bound.  Timing changes accrue through the action
indicators and durations, propagate through offsets and queues, and are
re-posed from measured state under \cref{eq:reopt}; a solution equal to the
launch plan everywhere is the well-formed no-action trajectory, and any
deviation is attributable to a named letter.

