\subsection{Decision Variables}
\label{sec:dvars}

\paragraph{One sentence first.}
The program's decision is, at every intersection $i$ and every integer step
$t$, \emph{which signal is green} --- and for the TSP programme that question
is asked of exactly two approaches: the \textbf{main} (bus/coordinated through
movement) and the \textbf{side} (cross-street) approaches.  Everything else in
this subsection is either a bookkeeping device for that one question, or a
state variable the objective reads.

\subsubsection{Indexing: approach, movement, phase, lane}
\label{sec:dvar_indexing}
The physical question ``is this signal open?'' is per \emph{approach} and, where
an approach splits, per \emph{movement}; it is answered for a whole
\emph{phase} at once, and its capacity is a per-\emph{lane} quantity.  Fixing
this vocabulary once is what makes the rest of the program readable, so we fix
it here:
\begin{longtable}{lp{10.2cm}}
\hline
Symbol & Definition \\
\hline
\endhead
\hline
\endfoot
$i \in \Isp$ & Intersection (a signalised node on the corridor) \\
$t \in \Tsp$ & Integer step, $\Delta t = 1$\,s \\
$p \in \Psp_i$ & \emph{Phase} at $i$: the unit the signal actually switches and times. One phase is green at a time (\cref{eq:gate_excl}), and a phase is a \emph{set} of compatible movements, not a single lane. \\
$m \in \Msp_i$ & \emph{Movement} (turning) at $i$, $m \in \{$main, side$\} \times \{$through, left, right$\}$ as the network provides. A movement belongs to exactly one approach. \\
$\sigma(m) \in \Ssp$ & The \emph{approach class} of movement $m$: $\text{main}$ if $m$ is on the coordinated through approach, else $\text{side}$. This is the main/side split the whole programme is written against: $D_{i,\text{main},\cdot}$ vs $D_{i,\text{side},\cdot}$, $\delta^{\text{eff}}_{i,\text{main},t}$ vs $\delta^{\text{eff}}_{i,\text{side},t}$, $x^{\text{main}}_{i,t}$ (\cref{eq:main_open}). \\
$\Psp_i(m) \ni p$ & The phase that serves movement $m$ at $i$. A signal opens movements in groups; $p$ is what is timed, $m$ is what is described. \\
$\Lsp_{i,m}$ & \emph{Lanes} serving movement $m$ at $i$, $\ell \in \Lsp_{i,m}$. Lanes carry capacity, not time: saturation is additive over lanes (\cref{eq:mu_lanes}), and no timing constraint is ever written per lane, because a lane is not independently signalled. \\
\end{longtable}

\subsubsection{Primary decision: the open/closed indicator}
The central decision variable is a \emph{binary openness} flag. We write it at
the phase level, because that is the granularity at which a signal switches,
and we make the main/side reading of it explicit immediately afterwards:
\begin{equation}
  x_{i,p,t} \;\in\; \{0,1\},
  \qquad
  x_{i,p,t} = 1 \;\Longleftrightarrow\;
  \text{phase } p \text{ is open (green) at } (i,t),
  \label{eq:x_primary}
\end{equation}
Read with respect to the \textbf{main movement}, this one variable has a
corridor name, and it is the object the entire objective acts through:
\begin{equation}
  x^{\text{main}}_{i,t} \;\triangleq\; x_{i,p_b^i,t},
  \qquad
  G^{\text{main}}_{i,c} \;\triangleq\; \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x^{\text{main}}_{i,t},
  \label{eq:x_main_reading}
\end{equation}
where $p_b^i$ is the bus-priority phase (the phase serving the main through
movement) and $W_{i,c}$ is a cycle window. So ``the main signal is open at
$(i,t)$'' is $x_{i,p_b^i,t} = 1$, and the side approaches are the complement
over the remaining phases. Delay is then attributed by approach class
$\sigma(\cdot)$, giving $D_{i,\text{main},v,t}$ and $D_{i,\text{side},v,t}$ from
the one flag, and lane capacity enters only through saturation.

