\subsection{Objective Function}
\label{sec:objectives}

The program has exactly one objective: it minimises total passenger delay.
Every other quantity is a cap or a monitor, never a second objective.  All
clocks are integer ($\Delta t = 1$); the delay quantities below are built from
the veh\,s variables $D_{i,s,v,t}$ of \cref{eq:mv_waitsum}, converted to
passenger-seconds by the occupancies $\rho_{\text{bus}} = 40$,
$\rho_{\text{car}} = 1.5$.  \cref{tab:objective_terms} is the objective
\emph{outline}: it lists the candidate terms a criterion of this kind could be
built from and states, for each, what it measures and the role it plays here.
The plan determines the objective: $Z_1$ is a composition of the program's own
maps (\cref{eq:timing_flow_chain}), so a timing plan is not an input to the
delay but the delay itself.
Only the first row is minimised; the remaining rows describe what a larger
program \emph{could} optimise and how the same terms are handled as caps or
monitors in this one.  The outline is a menu, not a switch: no term is a mode
and no second minimisation is nested inside the program.

