\paragraph{Minimised: total person delay ($Z_1$).}
Per-step veh\,s delay on section--class $(s,v)$ is $D_{i,s,v,t}$, which arises
from the movement queue recursion (\cref{eq:mv_q}) and the shut-gate waiting
rule (\cref{eq:mv_wait})---no linearised or fitted delay function is
involved.  Person delay is the single currency throughout: occupancy times
vehicle delay, $PD = \sum n_i d_i$.  With $\rho_v$ the occupancy (pax/veh)
of class $v$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:person_delay}
  P_{i,t} = \sum_{s \in \Ssp} \sum_{v \in \Vsp} \rho_v\, D_{i,s,v,t}
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp,
\end{equation}
in person-seconds; a bus carrying forty passengers counts forty times a
single-occupant car for the same seconds waited.  $Z_1$ is person delay with
an extra section weight $w_s$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z1}
  Z_1 = \sum_{t \in \Tsp} \sum_{i \in \Isp}
    \Bigl( w_{\text{main}}\,\rho_{\text{bus}}\, D_{i,\text{main},\text{bus},t}
    + w_{\text{main}}\,\rho_{\text{car}}\, D_{i,\text{main},\text{car},t}
    + w_{\text{side}}\,\rho_{\text{car}}\, D_{i,\text{side},\text{car},t} \Bigr).
\end{equation}
\paragraph{The estimated POG tracker stays online; the exact progression term is $Z_2$.}
The exact program does \emph{not} minimise the estimated Purdue POG tracker.
That tracker is a \emph{progression heuristic}: it is priced from an
\emph{estimated} POG state on measured data, it is not a delay quantity, and
it cannot be computed exactly without committing to a platoon decomposition
the program does not make. The online methods therefore keep it on the online
side, where it does real work: they add it to their \emph{action scores} to
break ties between candidates that $Z_1$ cannot separate, at weight
$w^{\text{POG}}$. Exact progression, by contrast, is $Z_2$
(\cref{eq:Z2}). It is a linear term that the modeller may add to the one
objective at a fixed coefficient $\omega_2 \ge 0$
(\cref{eq:opt_crit,tab:objective_terms}); with $\omega_2 = 0$ (the default) the
objective is passenger delay alone and $Z_2$ is monitored, not optimised. This
keeps the default exact objective a single, exactly-computed currency
(passenger delay) while leaving progression available as an exact add-on term
rather than only as an online heuristic. When no bus is present
($b^{\text{present}}_{i,t} = 0$, Sec.~\ref{sec:bus_optional}) the bus terms of
\cref{eq:Z1} vanish and $Z_1$ reduces to the car-only objective the
continuous monitor optimises online.

\paragraph{Progression term ($Z_2$).}
With integer mismatch $|\epsilon_{ij,t}| = \epsilon_{ij,t}^+ + \epsilon_{ij,t}^-$
and $\Delta_{\max} = 15$ steps:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z2}
  Z_2 = \sum_{t \in \Tsp} \sum_{(i,j) \in \Esp}
    q_{ij,t} \cdot \bigl(\Delta_{\max} - |\epsilon_{ij,t}|\bigr).
\end{equation}
Perfect offsets score $q\,\Delta_{\max}$, worst score zero; the progression
tolerance (Eq.\,\ref{eq:prog_tol}) keeps the schedule inside the green-wave
design bounds. By default $Z_2$ is monitored and not optimised; if the modeller
chooses to price progression it enters the one objective as $-\omega_2 Z_2$ at a
fixed coefficient $\omega_2$ (\cref{eq:opt_crit,tab:objective_terms}).

\paragraph{Capped, then penalised: schedule adherence ($Z_3$).}
Lateness is $\sigma_{b,i}^+ = \max(0, \tau_{b,i} - \bar{\tau}_{b,i})$
(\cref{eq:arr_prop,eq:sched_split}); earliness is free:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z3}
  Z_3 = \sum_{b \in \Bsp} \sum_{i \in \Rcal_b} \omega_{b}\, \sigma_{b,i}^+,
  \qquad \omega_b \ge 1.
\end{equation}
In scheduling terms $Z_3$ is total deadline violation and $Z_1$ total
flow-time cost.

\paragraph{Capped: corridor travel time ($Z_4$).}
Buses plus general traffic only (no trucks anywhere in the program).  With
$\mathcal{N}^v$ the class-$v$ vehicle set and integer steps
$t_n^{\text{entry}}$, $t_n^{\text{exit}}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z4}
  Z_4 = TT^{\text{bus}} + TT^{\text{car}}, \qquad
  TT^{v} = \sum_{n \in \mathcal{N}^v} \bigl(t_n^{\text{exit}} - t_n^{\text{entry}}\bigr),
  \quad v \in \{\text{bus},\text{car}\}.
\end{equation}
$Z_4$ is a post-run veh\,h outcome (network bus and car total-time
statistics, ``Total (h)'' in \cref{tab:totaltime}), never a per-step variable.

\subsubsection{Optimality Criterion}
\label{sec:objective_crit}
The program carries one minimised scalar. $\omega_2 \ge 0$ is a fixed
coefficient set before the solve---$\omega_2 = 0$ is the delay-only default and
any $\omega_2 > 0$ simply adds the $Z_2$ term of \cref{tab:objective_terms}---so
it is data, not a mode and not a tuned online weight:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:opt_crit}
  \min_{\text{schedule}}\; Z_1 - \omega_2 Z_2
  \quad \text{s.t.} \quad
  \text{physical feasibility (\cref{eq:start_def,eq:sched_cap}),}
  \quad
  \sigma^{+}_{b,i} \le \epsilon^{\text{late}} \;\; \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b,
  \quad
  Z_4 \le Z_4^{\text{fix}} + \varepsilon_{Z_4}.
\end{equation}
$Z_1$ equals the Bellman sum $\sum_t c_t$ of
\cref{sec:markovian}; apart from the fixed coefficient $\omega_2$,
there are no tuned composite weights in the exact program. The two caps work
as follows.  The \emph{schedule cap} demands every bus on time whenever the
timetable is attainable; under an upstream disruption that makes it
infeasible, the cap is relaxed and the same program falls back to minimising
$Z_1 + Z_3$ (aggregate lateness), staying feasible.  The \emph{throughput cap}
gives corridor service lexicographic priority: no delay saving may cost more
than $\varepsilon_{Z_4}$ of corridor vehicle-time over the fixed-time baseline
(90\,veh\,s in the exact statement; 300\,veh\,s in the scored
runs).  The all--no-action schedule always
satisfies physical feasibility and both caps, so the program degrades
gracefully to fixed time under saturation.

\paragraph{What the program does not screen.} The equations from
\cref{eq:start_def} to \cref{eq:sched_cap} state what the street allows;
no clause of them tests whether an action should be taken \emph{now}. Detection
epochs, the arrivals-on-green tracker, and the conditional-priority layer
(Sections~\ref{sec:bus_optional}, \ref{sec:rule_tsp}) therefore belong to the
solution algorithm, which applies them per proposal on estimated state, and to
neither exact optimum.

\subsection{Arrival uncertainty: what each part of the program must absorb}
\label{sec:stochastic}

\paragraph{Why the bounds do not settle it.}
The timing bounds of Sec.~\ref{sec:stage_a} are sized for the mean demand, so
the plan they admit is a mean plan---and a mean plan answers a question nobody
is asked, because the count that actually arrives is not the mean. Counting is a
discrete random process, so even at the mean rate the per-step count on a
movement fluctuates, and it is that fluctuation, not the mean, that decides
whether a queue forms behind a red. We therefore treat the arrival \emph{count}
as uncertain, and the substantive question is not ``what distribution'' but
\emph{which part of the program is responsible for absorbing it}. We state the
uncertainty and then, for each channel, the part that must carry the
consequence. This is deliberately \emph{not} a two-stage stochastic program:
the program is never solved here (Sec.~\ref{sec:milp_status}), so a
sample-average apparatus would claim a distributional guarantee we do not
establish. What follows is the design obligation each part inherits.

\paragraph{The uncertain count.}
Let $\bar{A}_{i,m}$ be the mean flow on movement $m$ at $i$ and
$\bar{A}_{i,m,t}$ its per-step mean. The realised count is
\begin{equation}
  A_{i,m,t} \;=\; \Bigl[\, \bar{A}_{i,m,t} + \xi_{i,m,t} \,\Bigr]^{\!\!+},
  \qquad \xi_{i,m,t} \sim \mathcal{N}\!\bigl(0,\ \bar{A}_{i,m,t} + \kappa\bigr),
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; m \in \Msp_i,\; t \in \Tsp,
\label{eq:arr_process}
\end{equation}
where $[\,\cdot\,]^{+}$ keeps the count non-negative and the variance is the
Poisson-like mean-plus-floor form for a count whose mean is small ($\kappa$
absorbing the deterministic OD-rate error at very low flows); the equivalent
low-count binomial $\mathrm{Bin}(n_{i,m,t},\pi_{i,m,t})$ is what the
implementation samples. Bus entries carry their own independent uncertainty,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:arr_error}
  \tau_{b,i_0} = \hat{\tau}_{b,i_0} + \epsilon_b,
  \qquad \epsilon_b \text{ Gaussian, mean zero, variance } \varsigma^2,
\end{equation}
with $\varsigma$ calibrated from ETA-tracker residuals---the same uncertainty
the online guards already carry as $\hat{\sigma}$. These are the only two
uncertain channels; everything downstream is deterministic given them.

\paragraph{What the uncertainty implies each part must do.}
\cref{tab:uncertainty} is the operative content of this subsection: for
each channel, the part of the program that absorbs it and the obligation that
follows. Three obligations recur and are worth naming, because they are what
makes the program correct under a fluctuating count rather than merely
well-defined at the mean:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Queue, not plan, carries the fluctuation.} A count above the
        mean does not change the plan; it changes $Q_{i,m,t}$
        (\cref{eq:mv_q}). Any part of the program that reads demand must
        therefore read the \emph{queue}, never $\bar{A}$---which is why
        $\delta^{\text{eff}}_{i,s,t}$ is defined from the forced-open count
        (\cref{eq:eff_main,eq:eff_side}) and not from flow.
  \item \textbf{One schedule, no foresight.} A single gate schedule must serve
        every realisation, so durations $\delta_{i,k}$ are chosen once and not
        revised per realisation. Because delay is nonlinear in the held
        duration, deciding the hold at the mean count is not the mean of the
        right decisions; the obligation is the conservative direction---over-
        rather than under-extend---and that is precisely the asymmetry the
        balance guard R1 (\cref{eq:rule_balance}, applied online in
        \cref{sec:rule_tsp}) enforces.
  \item \textbf{The lateness cap binds at the realised clock.} A bus that is on
        time at the predicted entry $\hat{\tau}_{b,i_0}$ is not on time at
        $\tau_{b,i_0}$ when $\varsigma > 0$. The cap
        $\sigma^{+}_{b,i} \le \epsilon^{\text{late}}$
        (\cref{eq:sched_cap}) is therefore an obligation on the
        \emph{realised} deviation, and a plan that meets it at the mean clock
        has no schedule-adherence guarantee at all.
\end{enumerate}

