\paragraph{Why not a stochastic program.}
A two-stage sample-average formulation is available and was considered: draw
$(\xi, \epsilon)$ jointly, commit $(x, y, s)$ nonanticipatively before the
draw, and minimise $\sum_s p_s Z_1^{s}$. We do not present it as a result
because it is not solved here, and a distributional guarantee from an unsolved
model is a weaker claim than an honest obligation list. The sample-average
construction and its convergence are standard
\citep{Shapiro2014Lectures}; \cref{tab:uncertainty} states what survives
without it, and every obligation in that table is enforced in the implemented
system---by the queue recursion, the R1 warrant, the realised-clock lateness
check, and the offset recovery of \cref{sec:rule_tsp}.

\subsection{Computational Status: Formulation Solved Nowhere, Benchmark Claimed Nowhere}
\label{sec:milp_status}

The word ``exact'' in this paper means \emph{exact formulation}, never
\emph{exactly solved benchmark}.  No MILP objective value, optimality gap,
solution time, node count, or branch-and-bound statistic is reported in this
paper, because none exists: the full-horizon program was never solved to
optimality on the Kelvin Grove corridor, and no number from it is used to
rank any controller.  All percentage changes in the Abstract and in
\cref{sec:results} are microsimulation outcomes (Aimsun Next,
\texttt{champion\_kg.csv}) measured against the \texttt{NO\_TSP} fixed-time
baseline, not against an MILP optimum.

\paragraph{Why it was not solved.}  At $\Delta t = 1$\,s the scored horizon is
$T = 4500$ steps (1.25\,h) over $|\Isp| = 12$ intersections with
$|\Psp_i| \approx 4$ phases each.  The time-expanded model therefore carries
roughly $|\Isp|\times|\Psp_i|\times T \approx 2.2\times10^5$ binary gate
variables $x_{i,p,t}$, $|\Isp|\times|\Ksp|\times T \approx 2.7\times10^5$
binary event variables $y_{i,k,t}$ plus an equal number of start variables
$s_{i,k,t}$, and of order $10^5$ integer queue, discharge, delay, offset, and
bus-clock variables, subject to of order $10^6$ constraints
(\cref{eq:excl,eq:rule_take}).  This is two orders of magnitude
beyond real-time exact solution on a 12-junction arterial.  We report the
program's scale and our non-solve explicitly instead.

\paragraph{What the MILP is then for.}  The program is the normative
definition of the control problem (what \emph{should} be done at every
intersection in every second under perfect prediction) and the common parent of
the Bellman recursion (Theorem~\ref{thm:milp_bellman} below) and the
single-event greedy subproblem the delay-based arms approximate
(\cref{sec:appendix_pseudo}); the online guard set and the rule layer
that inherit its physics inherit it through that subproblem rather than by
constraining it.  Its value is
definitional and architectural--one consistent model, one criterion, one
rule philosophy from which the delay-based arms inherit and against which
all fifteen scored arms are measured on the common microsimulation
criterion--not numerical.
No exact run complements the microsimulation comparison: the MILP minimises
\emph{predicted} delay under perfect foresight while the results report
\emph{realised} delay under stochastic demand.


