\subsubsection{Event Actions: Exclusivity, Integer Duration, and Contiguity}
The action at $(i,t)$ is an index $a_{i,t} \in \Ksp$ (the letter in effect,
with NA as explicit no-action); its one-hot encoding
$y_{i,k,t} = \mathbf{1}[a_{i,t} = k]$ is the form the linear constraints use, so
$y_{i,\text{GE},t}$ below is shorthand for ``the index at $(i,t)$ is GE''.
The full alphabet is
$\Ksp = \Acal = \{\text{GE}, \text{INS}, \text{ER}, \text{GR}, \text{VP},
\text{PT}, \text{OC}, \text{NA}\}$: the four gate-timing letters (extend,
insert, early-red, reallocate) plus the three \emph{plan-level} letters
(skip, rotate, offset-correct) that act on the phase sequence and offset
rather than a single gate, and no-action.  Every letter is carried by the
same index machinery below; only their gate footprint differs, which
\cref{eq:plan_gate} makes uniform.

\paragraph{Which intersection, and which phases.}
An action letter never acts on the corridor as a whole. The action variable
carries the intersection as its first index and is indexed per integer step:
$y_{i,k,t}$, $s_{i,k,t}$, and $\delta_{i,k}$ are all scoped to $i \in \Isp$, so
the program decides, at every intersection $i$ and every step $t$, which letter
is in effect \emph{there}. Intersections are therefore not linked by a single
shared action; each has its own action index and its own duration, and only the
coordination constraints of \cref{sec:coordination} couple them.

The gate footprint --- which phase gates the letter opens, extends, truncates or
reallocates --- is a per-intersection property, not a global constant, because
the phase set $\Psp_i$ and the conflicting pairs $\mathcal{F}_i$ differ between
junctions. Writing $g(i,k)$ for that footprint:

\begin{center}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{lllll}
\hline
Letter & Acts at & Gate footprint $g(i,k)$ & Duration index & Cycle effect \\
\hline
\texttt{GE} & $(i,t)$, bus/main phase $p_b(i)$ & extends $x_{i,p_b,t}$ onward & $\delta_{i,\text{GE}}$ & $+\delta$ \\
\texttt{INS} & $(i,t)$, phase $p_b(i)$ & inserts $x_{i,p_b}$ at $t$ & $\delta_{i,\text{INS}}$ & $+\delta$ \\
\texttt{ER} & $(i,t)$, bus/main phase $p_b(i)$ & truncates $x_{i,p_b}$ at $t$ & $\delta_{i,\text{ER}}$ & $-\delta$ \\
\texttt{GR} & $(i,t)$, phase $p_r(i)$ & shifts $\delta$ from $p_r$ to $p_m(i)$ & $\delta_{i,\text{GR}}$ & $0$ \\
\texttt{VP} & $(i,t)$, phase $p_s(i)$ & sets $x_{i,p_s}=0$ for the block & n/a & $-\lvert\text{skipped}\rvert$ \\
\texttt{PT} & $(i,t)$, whole $\Psp_i$ & reorders $\Psp_i$ next cycle & n/a & $0$ \\
\texttt{OC} & $(i,t)$, whole $\Psp_i$ & shifts every $x_{i,p,\cdot}$ in time & solved $\ge3$ cap $10$ & $0$ net \\
\texttt{NA} & $(i,t)$ & baseline plan & $0$ & $0$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Here $p_b(i)$ is the bus-priority phase, $p_m(i)$ the mainline phase, $p_r(i)$ a
reallocation donor phase, and $p_s(i)$ the skipped phase --- all read off
$\Psp_i$ at that junction, which is why a corridor-wide table cannot substitute
for $g(i,k)$. The first four letters have a per-intersection duration variable.
\texttt{PT} and \texttt{VP} commit a whole cycle and \texttt{NA} has none, which
is why those rows carry no $\delta_{i,k}$. Worked per-junction numbers are in
\cref{sec:worked_examples}.

Exactly one letter is in effect
per $(i,t)$; with $\Delta t = 1$ the held
duration of each letter is the count of active steps,
$\delta_{i,k} = \sum_t y_{i,k,t}$, held separately from the index.
No per-step fractional contribution exists.
\begin{align}
  \sum_{k \in \Ksp} y_{i,k,t} &= 1 && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:excl} \\[3pt]
  \delta_{i,k} &= \sum_{t \in \Tsp} y_{i,k,t} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; k \in \Ksp \label{eq:dur_int} \\[3pt]
  \bar{y}_{i,k} &= \sum_{t \in \Tsp} s_{i,k,t} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; k \in \Ksp \label{eq:ybar_def} \\[3pt]
  \delta_{i,k} &\ge \delta_k^{\min} \cdot \bar{y}_{i,k} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; k \in \Ksp \label{eq:bounds_min} \\[3pt]
  \delta_{i,k} &\le \delta_k^{\max} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; k \in \Ksp \label{eq:bounds_max}
\end{align}

\paragraph{Contiguity via start variables.}
Each action must run as one uninterrupted block and cannot be restarted later
in the horizon.  The start indicator and the two constraints that enforce
contiguity are
\begin{align}
  s_{i,k,t} &\ge y_{i,k,t} - y_{i,k,t-1}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; k \in \Ksp,\; t \in \Tsp \setminus \{0\} \label{eq:start_def} \\[3pt]
  s_{i,k,0} &= y_{i,k,0} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; k \in \Ksp \label{eq:start_init} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} s_{i,k,t} &\le 1 && \forall i \in \Isp,\; k \in \Ksp \label{eq:start_once} \\[3pt]
  s_{i,k,t} &\le y_{i,k,t} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; k \in \Ksp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:start_active}
\end{align}
Eq.\,\ref{eq:start_def}--\ref{eq:start_init} detect any rising edge of $y_{i,k,t}$.
Eq.\,\ref{eq:start_once} allows at most one start per action per
horizon.  Together these guarantee each action forms a single
contiguous integer block of length $\delta_{i,k}$.

\subsubsection{Gate-Opening Operations: What Changes Per Integer Step}
The heart of the time-expanded program is the \emph{operation schedule}: for
every intersection and every integer step, it decides which signal gate is
open, when the gates switch, and how long each stays open--so that the gates
open exactly when predicted vehicles (above all, buses) need to pass.
Let $x_{i,p,t} \in \{0,1\}$ indicate the gate of phase $p$ at intersection
$i$ is open during integer step $t$, and let $\mathcal{F}_i$ be the set of
conflicting phase pairs at $i$ ($\mathcal{F}_i$ is a pair set, distinct from the
cycle length $C_i$).  At most one gate per intersection at a time
(zero open during clearance), with tight signal-change accounting:
\begin{align}
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} x_{i,p,t} &\le 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_excl} \\[3pt]
  w^{\text{on}}_{i,p,t} - w^{\text{off}}_{i,p,t} &= x_{i,p,t} - x_{i,p,t-1}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \setminus \{0\} \label{eq:gate_switch} \\[3pt]
  w^{\text{on}}_{i,p,0} &= x_{i,p,0},\quad w^{\text{off}}_{i,p,0} = 0
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i \label{eq:gate_switch_init} \\[3pt]
  w^{\text{on}}_{i,p,t} + w^{\text{off}}_{i,p,t} &\le 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_edge} \\[3pt]
  x_{i,p',t} + x_{i,p,\,t-l} &\le 1
     && \forall i \in \Isp,\; (p,p') \in \mathcal{F}_i,\;
        l = 1,\dots,I_{\text{ig}} \label{eq:gate_clear} \\
\end{align}
Read $w^{\text{on}}$ as ``the gate just opened'' and $w^{\text{off}}$ as
``the gate just closed'': \cref{eq:gate_switch} sets their difference to
the change in $x$, \cref{eq:gate_edge} forbids both firing at once, so
exactly one of three cases holds at every step (opened, closed, unchanged).
The dwell \cref{eq:gate_dwell} then counts minimum-green seconds forward
from each $w^{\text{on}} = 1$.
\cref{eq:gate_clear} is the integer intergreen (amber/all-red)
clearance: after phase $p$ closes, a conflicting gate $p'$ may only open once
$I_{\text{ig}}$ integer steps have elapsed.  Opening a gate commits to a
minimum dwell (minimum green), so the schedule cannot flicker:
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{r=t}^{\min(t+G^{\min}_{i}-1,T)} x_{i,p,r}
  \;\ge\; G^{\min}_{i}\; w^{\text{on}}_{i,p,t}
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:gate_dwell}
\end{equation}

Gates convert open steps into passage.  With integer predicted arrivals
$A_{i,p,t}$ (clairvoyant perfect prediction over the horizon), the discharge
through an open gate and the queue it leaves behind evolve in whole vehicles:

\paragraph{Main-movement openness: the signal settings read at the main.}
The openness of a signal setting is its green interval per cycle, and the
quantity that matters for the corridor is the \emph{main movement}'s share of
it.  The main (coordinated through) movement is served exactly by the bus
phase $p_b^i$ -- the phase the controller treats as main, carrying the main
approach's turnings (the $[$\texttt{PHASEMAP}$]$ mapping of Sec.~\ref{eq:plan_gate})
-- so its openness is the bus-phase gate, and its per-cycle total is the bus
green:
\begin{align}
  x^{\text{main}}_{i,t} &\;\triangleq\; x_{i,p_b^i,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:main_open} \\[3pt]
  G^{\text{main}}_{i,c} &\;\triangleq\; \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x^{\text{main}}_{i,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; c = 1,\dots,\lceil T/C_i \rceil \label{eq:main_green}
\end{align}
Equations~\ref{eq:x_primary}--\ref{eq:x_main_reading} state this same
definition up front, in Sec.~\ref{sec:dvars}; it is repeated here so the
gate block reads without a forward reference.  Saturation, the one place lanes
enter, is additive over the lanes serving a phase:
\begin{equation}
  \mu_{i,p} \;=\; \sum_{m:\,\Psp_i(m) = p} \;\sum_{\ell \in \Lsp_{i,m}} \mu_{i,\ell},
  \qquad
  A_{i,p,t} \;=\; \sum_{m:\,\Psp_i(m) = p} A_{i,m,t},
  \label{eq:mu_lanes}
\end{equation}
so a phase inherits the capacity of its lanes and the demand of its movements,
and no timing constraint is ever written per lane.
The signal settings are therefore read \emph{with respect to the main
movement} as the plan's gate profile $x^{\text{main}}_{i,t}$: the plan
$\bar{P}$ fixes its baseline (the fixed-time openness), each action
perturbs it ($\texttt{GE}/\texttt{INS}$ extend $x^{\text{main}}$,
$\texttt{ER}$ truncates it, $\texttt{GR}$ reallocates openness into it,
$\texttt{VP}/\texttt{PT}/\texttt{OC}$ move when it occurs, via
\cref{eq:plan_gate}), and every main/side delay split
($D_{i,\text{main},\cdot,\cdot}$ vs $D_{i,\text{side},\cdot,\cdot}$, and the
effective forcing of \cref{eq:eff_main}) is taken against this one gate.
This is the object the online controller reads live: the bus phase, its
remaining green, and the main approach's x-ratio
(Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards}) are all $x^{\text{main}}_{i,t}$ in one form
or another.  With integer predicted arrivals
$A_{i,p,t}$ (clairvoyant perfect prediction over the horizon), the discharge
through an open gate and the queue it leaves behind evolve in whole vehicles:
\begin{align}
  g_{i,p,t} &= x_{i,p,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_green} \\[3pt]
  d_{i,p,t} &\le \mu_{i,p}\, g_{i,p,t}, \qquad
  d_{i,p,t} \le Q_{i,p,t} + A_{i,p,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_discharge} \\[3pt]
  Q_{i,p,t+1} &= Q_{i,p,t} + A_{i,p,t} - d_{i,p,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \setminus \{T\} \label{eq:gate_queue}
\end{align}
with $Q_{i,p,0}$ given and consistent with \cref{eq:q_phase_sum}.
\cref{eq:gate_discharge} caps discharge by
saturation flow over the open step and by the vehicles actually present; the
queue recursion (\cref{eq:gate_queue}) carries unserved vehicles forward.

Both are summed consequences of the movement level, not independent decisions:
$d_{i,p,t}=\sum_{m:p(m)=p}d_{i,m,t}$ is held by definition (Sec.~\ref{sec:dvars}
variable table), so the discharge actually decided is $d_{i,m,t}$ under
\cref{eq:mv_dcap},
and the phase equations restate what those decisions imply in aggregate ---
and the delay variables lower-bound the area under the predicted queue
($D_{i,s,v,t} \ge \sum_{p \in \Psp_i(s,v)} Q_{i,p,t}$ up to integer
initial-condition constants), so \emph{delay arises exactly from gates being
closed while predicted arrivals wait}.  Opening the right gates at the right
integer steps is therefore the sole mechanism by which the schedule acts on
the objective.  This is the single definition of $g$; there is no second
baseline-plus-$\delta$ definition.

\subsubsection{Subsubsection~\ref{ssec:cons}: the missing conservation law,
and the signal setting that drives it}
\label{ssec:cons}

\paragraph{The hole, stated first.} \cref{eq:gate_green,eq:gate_queue}
are a closed recursion in $Q$ and $d$ \emph{given} $A_{i,p,t}$, and
$A_{i,p,t}$ is, at this point in the program, an input: it is described as
``clairvoyant perfect prediction'' but no constraint generates it. Without a
conservation law the program can pick a schedule that never has to cope with
where vehicles actually came from, and every corridor quantity downstream of it
(the offsets of Sec.~\ref{sec:coordination}, the plateau objective
$Z_2$) inherits the gap. This subsubsection supplies the law and, with it, the
one thing the rest of the program treats as fixed but a retiming layer should
not: the base plan settings themselves.

\paragraph{Conservation across a link.} Let $\Esp$ be the directed links and,
for each $(i,j)\in\Esp$, let $\tau^0_{ij}\in\Z_{\ge1}$ be the \emph{integer
free-flow} crossing time. Traffic entering link $(i,j)$ at step $t$ arrives at
$j$'s back of queue at $t+\tau^0_{ij}$, so with $n_{ij,t}\in\Z_{\ge0}$ the
exogenous demand released onto link $(i,j)$ at $t$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cons_link}
  N^{\text{in}}_{ij,t} \;=\; \sum_{t' \le t - \tau^0_{ij}}
    \Bigl( n_{ij,t'} + \!\!\sum_{p\in\Psp_i} d_{i,p,t'}\,\gamma^{p}_{i\to j} \Bigr)
  \quad \forall (i,j)\in\Esp,\; t\in\Tsp,
\end{equation}
and $A_{i,p,t}$, the per-phase inflow the recursion of
\cref{eq:gate_queue} consumes, is that inflow split across the phases that
actually serve it:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cons_phase}
  A_{i,p,t} \;=\; \sum_{(k,i)\in\Esp,\, p\in\Psp_{k\to i}}
    \gamma^{p}_{k\to i}\, N^{\text{in}}_{ki,t} \;+\; n^{p}_{i,t}
  \quad \forall i\in\Isp,\; p\in\Psp_i,\; t\in\Tsp .
\end{equation}
$\gamma^{p}_{i\to j}\in\{0,1\}$ is fixed geometry---which movements of link
$(i,j)$ are served by phase $p$ at $i$---so it carries no decision content and
\cref{eq:cons_link} is a \emph{constant-lag} linear recursion. Together with
\cref{eq:gate_queue} this is the discrete point-queue network loading
relation: what leaves $i$ at $t$ is what reaches $j$ at $t+\tau^0_{ij}$, and
nothing is created at $j$.

\paragraph{Why not $D_i(t)=s_i(t)A_i(t)$ directly.} The natural first attempt is
to write discharge as a green indicator times arrivals, $D_i(t)=s_i(t)A_i(t)$
with $s_i(t)=\mathbb 1\{\text{green}\}$, and move the flow forward as
$A_j(t+\tau_{ij})=D_i(t)$. It is the right \emph{idea} and it is already
contained above, but written literally it degenerates, and the reason is worth
recording because the degenerate form is attractive to implement. If discharge
is unbounded green times arrivals, then every vehicle that is present during any
green step leaves during it, so delay is zero whenever a gate is open and the
objective is minimised by opening everything---the program returns ``all green''
and the coordination terms have nothing to trade. The fix is already in
\cref{eq:gate_discharge}: discharge is capped by saturation flow
$\mu_{i,p}$, not by arrival. So the green indicator belongs in the program as
the \emph{capacity} switch $\mu_{i,p}g_{i,p,t}$, not as a multiplier on demand,
and it is $g$---not a free $s$---that the plan sets. The two differences matter:
the propagation stays exact, and saturation is what makes red cost something.

\paragraph{The signal setting, promoted to decisions.} In the rest of the
program the base plan is data: the cycle length $C_i$ is a parameter and the
phase skeleton is fixed. For a \emph{retiming}
layer that is exactly the part that should move, so it is promoted, with hats to
keep it distinct from the fixed parameters used elsewhere:
$\hat{C}_i\in\Z$ the cycle length, $\hat{G}_{i,p}\in\Z$ the split (green) of
phase $p$, and $\hat{O}_i\in\Z$ the offset. Write $p_1,\dots,p_{\mathrm{last}}$
for the phases of $\Psp_i$ in their given cyclic order, and
$I_{i,p}\ge0$ for the given intergreen, so the
green budget is conserved and the offset has one representative:
\begin{align}
  \hat{G}_{i,p} &\ge G^{\min}_{i,p}, \qquad \hat{G}_{i,p} \le G^{\max}_{i,p}
    && \forall i\in\Isp,\; p\in\Psp_i \label{eq:set_min} \\[3pt]
  \textstyle\sum_{p\in\Psp_i}\bigl(\hat{G}_{i,p} + I_{i,p}\bigr) &= \hat{C}_i
    && \forall i\in\Isp \label{eq:set_budget} \\[3pt]
  C^{\min}_i \le \hat{C}_i &\le C^{\max}_i, \qquad
  0 \le \hat{O}_i \le \hat{C}_i - 1
    && \forall i\in\Isp \label{eq:set_cycle} \\[3pt]
  \hat{O}_{i_0} &= 0
    && \text{(offset gauge: fixes the modulo redundancy)} \label{eq:set_gauge}
\end{align}
With the order given, phase $p$ opens at the within-cycle position
$\sigma_{i,p}=\sum_{p'<p}\bigl(\hat{G}_{i,p'}+I_{i,p'}\bigr)$, and the gate is
open on cycle $k$ over $[k\hat{C}_i+\hat{O}_i+\sigma_{i,p},\, k\hat{C}_i+\hat{O}_i+\sigma_{i,p}+\hat{G}_{i,p})$.
That window is imposed by the \emph{same} assignment pattern already used for
bus arrivals in \cref{eq:arr_assign}---an indicator selects the containing
step, so no modular arithmetic and no free binary survives: introducing
$z_{i,p,k,t}\in\{0,1\}$ and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:set_window}
\begin{aligned}
  \sum_{t} z_{i,p,k,t} &= \hat{G}_{i,p},
    && \forall i\in\Isp,\; p\in\Psp_i,\; k, \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t} t\, z_{i,p,k,t} &= k\hat{C}_i + \hat{O}_i + \sigma_{i,p},
    && \forall i\in\Isp,\; p\in\Psp_i,\; k, \\[3pt]
  g_{i,p,t} &\ge \sum_{k} z_{i,p,k,t},
    && \forall i\in\Isp,\; p\in\Psp_i,\; t\in\Tsp .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
the gate profile is a \emph{consequence} of $(\hat{O},\hat{G},\hat{C})$, not an
independent binary field. Note what this does and does not buy. It does not
remove variables: the $z$ are still of order $|\Isp|\,|\Psp_i|\,|\Tsp|$. What it
removes is the solver's \emph{search}: the program no longer explores a gate
open/close pattern over an exponential space with the settings as a by-product,
but instead searches $3|\Isp| + \sum_i|\Psp_i|$ integers and lets the gate
follow. That is what makes a solver claim conceivable here, and it is a
different (and smaller) benefit than a variable-count reduction---the reductions
that do move the count are collected in
Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_viability}.

\paragraph{The delay-coupled travel time, and why it stays out.} One may want
$\tau_{ij}=\tau^0_{ij}+\alpha\,\mathrm{delay}_{ij}(\hat{O},\hat{G},\hat{C})$ so
that the setting itself bends the arrival curve. This is not MILP-representable
as written: $\tau_{ij}$ is then a decision and $A_j(t+\tau_{ij})=D_i(t)$
indexes one constraint by a variable, which is bilinear at best. Three
possibilities, in decreasing order of preference. (i) \emph{Fixed propagation,
delay in the objective} (adopted in \cref{eq:cons_link}): $\tau^0$ is a
constant, the recursion is linear, and the setting's delay effect is already
counted---once as $Q_{i,p,t}$ in $Z_1$ and, for buses, through the timetable
term $Z_3$. $\alpha$ then earns its keep as an \emph{estimator}, fitted by
least squares on the observed relation between realised link crossing time and
the realised signal delay, and used to (a) validate that $\tau^0$ plus modelled
delay reproduces the measured $\tau$, and (b) supply $\hat{\tau}$ to the offline
progression solve of the offline progression solve. This is the ``practical
trick'' of calibrating on observed bus departures, and it is worth stating
plainly that it is a \emph{calibration and validation device, not a
constraint}. (ii) \emph{Scenario enumeration}: restrict
$\mathrm{delay}_{ij}$ to a small set $\{\xi_1,\dots,\xi_K\}$ of integer levels
and solve $K$ MILPs, taking the best; linear, and affordable while $K\lesssim5$.
(iii) Continuous $\tau_{ij}$: not attempted. Bus arrivals are already carried
deterministically by \cref{eq:arr_prop}, which is the mechanism that makes
the setting bite on them; car arrivals use (i).

\paragraph{Pricing progression, in add-on form.} If the modeller chooses to
price progression, it is added to the \emph{single} objective of
\cref{eq:opt_crit} as a fixed-coefficient linear term---never a second program
and never a nested minimisation. Let
$b^{\text{tt}}_{i,t}\in\{0,1\}$ mark the step at which a timetabled bus is at
$i$'s stop line---a \emph{conventional, scheduled} arrival, so deterministic,
with no distributional assumption anywhere---and let
$\mathrm{POG}_i$ be the fraction of those buses that meet an open bus gate:
\begin{align}
  \mathrm{POG}_i &= \frac{1}{\textstyle\sum_{t} b^{\text{tt}}_{i,t}}
     \sum_{t} b^{\text{tt}}_{i,t}\, g_{i,b(i),t}
    && \forall i\in\Isp \label{eq:ret_pog} \\[3pt]
  Z_2^{\text{add}} &= \omega^{\text{POG}} \textstyle\sum_i w_i
    \bigl(1-\mathrm{POG}_i\bigr) + \omega^{\text{TV}} \sum_{i,t,p}
    \bigl| g_{i,p,t+1} - g_{i,p,t} \bigr|
    \label{eq:ret_obj}
\end{align}
with $b(i)$ the bus-priority phase and $w_i$ a weighting (uniform reproduces
plain bandwidth). Both terms are linear in $g$ once
$\sum_t b^{\text{tt}}_{i,t}$ is fixed by the timetable, and the total-variation
penalty needs only the standard auxiliary variable per absolute value. The TV
term is not cosmetic: without it, a criterion whose only progression reward is
arrivals-on-green will happily alternate the offset between adjacent steps,
which is optimal in the model and absurd in the street. Adding
$Z_2^{\text{add}}$ to the objective of \cref{eq:opt_crit} \emph{is} the whole of
``pricing progression'': the program remains the minimisation of one scalar,
the chatter-free schedule claim of Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards} is enforced by
$\omega^{\text{TV}}$ rather than asserted, and no minimisation is nested inside
the program.

\paragraph{Why these added terms are deterministic.} The section on
arrival uncertainty (Sec.~\ref{sec:stochastic}) argues the full program should
not become stochastic; these add-on terms make that concrete. Buses enter at
their scheduled times as data, cars enter through $n_{ij,t}$, and nothing in
\cref{eq:cons_link,eq:ret_obj} samples, hedges, or penalises a
distribution. All stochasticity---and all of the measurement error in
$\alpha$---is pushed to the online layer of
Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards}, which is where the microsimulation results of
Sec.~\ref{sec:results} are actually produced. A deterministic pricing layer
and a stochastic recourse layer are complementary: the first says where the
schedule should sit, the second says what to do when the buses do not.

The TSP event actions act on this operation schedule only through the coupling
below: Green Extension and Phase Insertion force additional bus-gate-open
steps (Eqs.~\ref{eq:couple_ge}, \ref{eq:couple_pi}), Early Red forces early
closure of a conflicting gate (\cref{eq:couple_er}), and Green Reallocation
moves open steps from a donor gate to the bus gate within the same cycle
(\cref{eq:couple_gr}).  The action start/contiguity variables
(\cref{eq:start_def,eq:start_active}) are the action-start logic of
the TSP event layer, nested inside the per-step operation backbone above.

\subsubsection{Action-to-Gate Coupling: the chosen action is a plan (exact, recursive)}
The old per-letter forcing (``GE adds bus-gate steps, PI adds steps, ER
closes a gate, GR moves a step'') cannot describe the real alphabet: VP skips
phases, PT reorders them, OC shifts the offset, recovery trims shorten cross
phases -- none is ``hold one gate open''.  Every action, however, has the
same footprint: it produces a candidate \emph{phase plan} at $i$ -- the green
durations $g_{i,p}$, their sequence, and the offset $\phi_i$ -- and the
schedule maps any plan to its gate profile $\bar{x}^{P}_{i,p,t} \in \{0,1\}$
(a \emph{parameter}: gate of phase $p$ open at step $t$ under plan $P$,
including intergreens).  The chosen action is therefore a plan selection,
not a per-gate forcing.  Concretely:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \texttt{GE}$_\delta$: baseline plan with $g_{i,p_b} \leftarrow
        g_{i,p_b} + \delta$; \texttt{INS}$_\delta$: a $\delta$-step bus phase
        inserted into the sequence; \texttt{ER}$_\delta$: bus green truncated
        by $\delta$; \texttt{GR}$_\delta$: $\delta$ steps moved from a donor
        phase to the bus phase (cycle conserved);
  \item \texttt{VP}$_{p^*}$: the plan that skips to phase $p^*$
        (intermediate phases served zero green); \texttt{PT}: a reordered
        candidate sequence; \texttt{OC}$_{+\Delta}$: the baseline shifted by
        $\Delta$ steps; \texttt{NA}: the baseline plan itself.
\end{itemize}
Every perturbation above carries its own clearance: $T_{\text{INS}}$ inserts
the $\delta$-step bus phase \emph{between} two $I_{\text{ig}}$-step clearance
intervals (current-to-bus and bus-to-next), $T_{\text{PT}}$ preserves
$I_{\text{ig}}$ clearance across every new adjacency the reorder creates, and
$T_{\text{VP}}$ advances only after the current intergreen completes.  Swaps
and inserts are therefore feasible by construction of the parameter
$\bar{x}^P$, not by a constraint the solver must discover: an inserted or
reordered plan that could not clear is never generated as a candidate
$(i,a,\delta)$ triple.  The intergreen set $\mathcal{F}_i$ in
\cref{eq:gate_clear} then applies uniformly to baseline and perturbed plans
alike, because every plan in the alphabet already internalises its own
switching physics before the solver sees it.
Signal groups enter here, not only phases: each phase serves a set of signal
groups $\mathcal{S}_{i,p}$ and each SG a set of movements
$\mathcal{M}_{i,m}$ (turnings with their lanes, the movement view of
Sec.~\ref{eq:mv_q}); an SG is green under plan $P$ on the union of its
phases' green intervals, so the gate profile is equivalently written per SG,
$x^{\text{sg}}_{i,m,t} = \bigvee_{p \in \mathcal{S}_{i,p} \ni m} x_{i,p,t}$,
and delay is counted per SG (\cref{eq:mv_waitsum}).  A GE extends the SG
serving the bus; a VP/PT changes which SGs are green when; a GR moves green
between SGs.  The chosen action selects one plan, and the schedule is the
plan's gates.

\paragraph{From a plan to its gate profile, in closed form.}
The coupling below is only meaningful if the profile $\bar{x}^{P}$ is
determined by the plan rather than asserted alongside it, so we state the map
explicitly.  A plan $P$ at intersection $i$ is the quadruple
$P = (\sigma^P_i,\; g^P_{i,\cdot},\; C^P_i,\; \varphi^P_i)$: an order
$\sigma^P_i$ on $\Psp_i$, a green duration $g^P_{i,p}\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$ per
phase, the induced cycle $C^P_i = \sum_{p\in\Psp_i} g^P_{i,p} + L_i$, and an
offset $\varphi^P_i$.  Write the cumulative service already delivered before the
$j$-th phase of the order as
\begin{equation}
  G^P_{i,\sigma^P_i(j)} \;=\; \sum_{j' < j} g^P_{i,\sigma^P_i(j')}
  \qquad j = 1,\dots,|\Psp_i| ,
  \label{eq:plan_prefix}
\end{equation}
so $G^P_{i,\sigma^P_i(j)}$ is the step at which that phase's green begins
within the cycle.  The gate profile of $P$ is then a closed-form function of
$(P,t)$ and of nothing else:
\begin{equation}
  \bar{x}^{P}_{i,p,t}
  \;=\; \sum_{j=1}^{|\Psp_i|}
    \mathbf{1}\!\left\{\,p = \sigma^P_i(j)\,\right\}\,
    \mathbf{1}\!\left\{\,
      \bigl(t - \varphi^P_i\bigr) \bmod C^P_i
      \;\in\; \bigl[\,G^P_{i,\sigma^P_i(j)},\;
                   G^P_{i,\sigma^P_i(j)} + g^P_{i,\sigma^P_i(j)}\,\bigr)
    \right\},
  \label{eq:plan_profile}
\end{equation}
that is, the gate of phase $p$ is open at $t$ exactly when the modulo clock
falls inside $p$'s green interval, the interval being located by the prefix sum
of \cref{eq:plan_prefix} along the plan's own order.  This is a definition,
not a fitted profile: given $(P,t)$ it is evaluable with no further data, and it
is the only place the plan enters the timing.

The letter-indexed plan is obtained from the launch baseline $P^0_i$ by a
per-letter perturbation map $T_a$, so that
\begin{equation}
  P(i,a,\tau) \;=\; T_a\!\left(P^0_i,\;\delta_{i,a}\right),
  \qquad a \in \Acal,\;\; k \in \Ksp ,
  \label{eq:plan_select}
\end{equation}
i.e.\ $T_a$ adds $\delta_{i,a}$ to the bus-phase green for \texttt{GE}, inserts a
$\delta_{i,a}$-step bus phase for \texttt{INS}, subtracts it for \texttt{ER},
transfers $\delta_{i,a}$ from a donor phase for \texttt{GR}, and leaves order or
offset untouched for \texttt{VP}/\texttt{PT}/\texttt{OC}; $T_{\text{NA}}$ is the
identity.  Because $\delta_{i,a}$ ranges over the integers of
$[\delta_a^{\min},\delta_a^{\max}]$, \cref{eq:plan_prefix,eq:plan_profile}
are evaluated once per admissible duration, giving $\bar{x}^P$ the
parameter status the coupling requires: a $0/1$ constant indexed by
$(i,a,\delta)$, not a decision variable and not a function of $t$ beyond the
modulo clock.  Substituting gives the two constraints that tie the letters to
the gate profile---one letter in effect per step, and the realised gate a
superposition of the selected plans' profiles:
\begin{align}
  \sum_{a \in \Acal} y_{i,a,t} &= 1 && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:alphabet} \\[3pt]
  x_{i,p,t} &\;\ge\; \sum_{a \in \Acal}\ \sum_{\tau \le t}
    s_{i,a,\tau}\; \bar{x}^{T_a(P^0_i,\delta_{i,a})}_{i,p,\,t-\tau}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:plan_gate}
\end{align}
\cref{eq:plan_gate} is therefore a composition of three stated maps,
$(a,\delta)\mapsto P\mapsto\bar{x}^P\mapsto x$, and nothing else: the
$\tau$-sum shifts each plan profile to the step at which its action started, and
\cref{eq:alphabet} makes the sum a partition, so at every $(i,t)$ exactly one
term contributes. The gate is thus a function of the chosen letter and its
duration alone --- with \emph{no} feasibility screen applied to that choice,
which is what separates this coupling from the guards of
\cref{sec:online_guards}.  This has one sharp consequence the reader
should not miss: a letter whose plan violates min-green, intergreen, or
spillback does not get priced --- it makes the program \emph{infeasible}.
Infeasibility here is the exact counterpart of an online guard veto: the solver
reports impossibility where the controller refuses to attempt. Any comparison
between the clairvoyant bound and a controller's realised delay is therefore a
comparison between ``the best feasible plan'' and ``the best plan the guards
let through,'' never between a priced and an unpriced infeasibility.  Delay then propagates from $x$ through the
single queue recursion (\cref{eq:gate_queue,eq:gate_discharge}) for
\emph{every} letter alike.  This is the ``sum through the chosen action'' the
online controller
performs: one mechanism (action $\to$ plan $\to$ SG gates $\to$ queue $\to$
delay), not a letter-specific coefficient.  GR conserves the cycle and OC
carries no net cycle change, so both leave the debt-free offset behaviour of
\cref{eq:offset_repay}; VP/PT pay their offset cost through the same
lagged mismatch.  The product $s_{i,a,\tau}\bar{x}^{P}$ is a binary times a
fixed $0/1$ parameter (no bilinearity); a NA start selects the baseline plan
and adds nothing.  Hold/contiguity
(\cref{eq:hold_chain,eq:one_stop}) apply to the action start
$s_{i,a,\tau}$ unchanged.  All actions are unsolvable in general: the
enumeration of candidate plans induced by \cref{eq:plan_select}, on top of
integer grids, puts exact solution out of reach at corridor scale.  The program
cannot actually be solved, only stated (Sec.~\ref{sec:milp_status}); its
bounds are the feasibility envelope.  For the record, the tractability
condition is finiteness of the alphabet--duration grid: the modulo clock in
\cref{eq:plan_profile} never enters the solver because $\bar{x}^P$ is
precomputed over the finite set $\Acal\times\prod_a[\delta_a^{\min},
\delta_a^{\max}]$; an unbounded or continuous duration set would make the
parameter table infinite and the program not even statable as a MILP, let
alone solvable.  What enacts actions instead is the
solution algorithms -- choosing the index $a_{i,t}$, sizing $\delta$, and
predicting the arrivals it will serve -- all online
(Secs.~\ref{sec:solutions}--\ref{sec:selection_layer}; executable versions
in \cref{sec:appendix_pseudo}), within the holds and bounds above.
What the code additionally checks before allowing each letter (detection
window, live phase, cooldown, spillback, congestion, veto, Purdue gate) is
not part of this coupling; it is the online guard set of
\cref{sec:online_guards}.

\paragraph{Per-letter consequences as corollaries.} \cref{eq:plan_gate} alone
fixes the gates, so each per-letter statement below is a \emph{projection} of that
equation rather than an additional constraint: substituting the closed form of
\cref{eq:plan_profile} for each $T_a$ and collecting the terms in which
$\mathbf{1}\{p = \sigma^P_i(j)\}$ selects a single phase yields it. We retain the
original labels because they are the classical statements each is derived from.
Writing $p_b^i$ for the bus phase of $\Psp_i$ and $\Psp_i^c$ for its complement:
\begin{align}
  x_{i,p_b^i,t} &\ge y_{i,\text{GE},t} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:couple_ge} \\[3pt]
  x_{i,p_b^i,t} &\ge y_{i,\text{INS},t} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:couple_pi} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i^c} x_{i,p,t} &\le |\Psp_i^c|\,(1 - y_{i,\text{ER},t}) && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:couple_er} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} x_{i,p,t} - \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} \bar{x}_{i,p,t} &\le M^{\text{big}}\,(1 - y_{i,\text{GR},t}) && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:couple_gr} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} \bar{x}_{i,p,t} - \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} x_{i,p,t} &\le M^{\text{big}}\,(1 - y_{i,\text{GR},t}) && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:couple_gr2}
\end{align}
Equations~\ref{eq:couple_ge}--\ref{eq:couple_pi} are the one-sided form of the
identity $x_{i,p,t} = \sum_{a,\tau} s_{i,a,\tau}\bar{x}^{T_a}_{i,p,t-\tau}$: both
directions hold, but only the lower bound is written, because the upper bound
adds nothing once \cref{eq:gate_excl} already caps $\sum_p x_{i,p,t}$ at one.
\begin{lemma}[One-hot profile]
For every plan $P$ generated by any $T_a$, the profile $\bar{x}^P_{i,\cdot,t}$
is one-hot over $p\in\Psp_i$ at every $t$: exactly one phase shows green.
\emph{Proof.} By \cref{eq:plan_profile}, $\bar{x}^P_{i,p,t}=1$ iff the modulo
clock falls in $p$'s green interval $[G^P_{i,p},G^P_{i,p}+g^P_{i,p})$, where the
intervals $\{G^P_{i,\sigma^P_i(j)},G^P_{i,\sigma^P_i(j)}+g^P_{i,\sigma^P_i(j)}\}_j$
partition $[0,C^P_i)$ by construction of the prefix sums in
\cref{eq:plan_prefix} along the plan's own order $\sigma^P_i$.  Exactly one
interval contains any clock value, so exactly one $p$ shows green. \qed
\end{lemma}
Together with \cref{eq:alphabet} (exactly one letter active per $(i,t)$),
the lemma gives that \cref{eq:plan_gate}'s lower bound plus
\cref{eq:gate_excl} determine $x$ fully: the forced $1$ occupies the single
allowed open slot, so every other phase is $0$ with no upper-bound constraint
needed.
\cref{eq:couple_er} is the same reading for a truncation: $\delta_{i,\text{ER}}$
steps of $p_b^i$ are removed from the end of its green interval, so on those steps
the prefix sum of \cref{eq:plan_prefix} has already advanced past every
remaining phase and no cross gate can open. The pair
\cref{eq:couple_gr,eq:couple_gr2} states conservation rather than
shape: because $T_{\text{GR}}$ preserves $C_i$ and transfers
$\delta_{i,\text{GR}}$ between phases, the open-step total over the cycle is
unchanged, which is exactly the two-sided bound on
$\sum_p x_{i,p,t} - \sum_p \bar{x}_{i,p,t}$; the big-$M$ deactivates both when
$y_{i,\text{GR},t} = 0$, leaving the baseline profile free.

Because these follow from \cref{eq:plan_gate}, adding or removing any of them
does not change the feasible set; they are stated because they are the form in
which the classical literature recognises each letter.
The granted letter is \emph{held}, not flickered, exactly as before:
\begin{align}
  s_{i,k,t} &\le y_{i,k,\tau} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; k \in \Ksp,\; t \in \Tsp,\; \tau = t,\dots,\min(t + \delta_k^{\min} - 1, T) \label{eq:hold_chain} \\[3pt]
  y_{i,a,t} - y_{i,a,t+1} &\le f_{i,a,t} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; a \in \Ksp,\; t \in \Tsp \setminus \{T\} \label{eq:no_flicker} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} f_{i,a,t} &\le 1 && \forall i \in \Isp,\; a \in \Ksp \label{eq:one_stop}
\end{align}
with stop indicator $f_{i,a,t} \in \{0,1\}$ (dropping a letter forces $f=1$
at the drop step, at most one drop), so every grant is one contiguous hold
of $\delta_{i,a} \in [\delta_a^{\min}, \delta_a^{\max}]$ steps -- decided
inside the program by the start/stop pair, never preset.

\paragraph{Reading the program as a schedule: ordering, timing, and actions.}
The construction has the shape of a machine-schedule: each phase is a
\emph{server} that releases one movement class at a time, and a vehicle
wanting that movement is a \emph{demand unit} whose passage at the junction is
to be served by a green interval.  Three things are the schedule, and the
actions are the perturbations of it:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Ordering} -- the phase sequence and the plan-level letters
        (VP skip, PT rotation) \emph{reorder the service}: which movement
        class is released before which.  This is the disjunctive order at each
        intersection.
  \item \textbf{Timing} -- the green durations $g_{i,p}$, the cycle, and the
        offset $\phi_i$ are the server \emph{service times, cycle time, and
        release offset}.  GR moves service time between phases; OC shifts the
        release offsets; recovery trims back borrowed time.
  \item \textbf{Actions} -- GE extends a phase's service interval to fit a
        late demand unit; INS inserts an unscheduled phase (a new operation);
        ER releases a phase early once its demand has cleared.  Every action
        is a bounded, integer perturbation of the schedule, and the
        delay/queue recursion (\cref{eq:mv_q}) is the flow through the
        perturbed schedule -- the ``sum through the chosen action'' of
        \cref{eq:plan_gate}.
\end{itemize}
The corridor couples the per-junction machine schedules through the offset
state (\cref{eq:offset_repay}): the release offset of machine $p$ at
junction $j$ is set so the platoon leaving machine $p'$ at $i$ arrives as
$j$ opens its corresponding machine, the setup (intergreen) acting as the
transfer/setup time between machines.  A single GE at $i$ thus delays the
release offset of every downstream machine by the same amount until
recovery trims it back -- the machine-schedule version of the green-wave
coupling.  This is the reading that makes the exact program a genuine
schedule, and it is the one the online algorithms approximate greedily one
epoch at a time.

\paragraph{The timing-to-flow chain as a stated composition.} A timing change is
not an input to the delay; it \emph{is} the delay, through a chain of four maps
that this section writes once so it cannot be missed.  Writing $P_i$ for the
committed timing plan at $i$ (phase durations, order and offset), the chain is
the following four identities, each of which is a constraint already imposed
above, so that together they are a program in $P_i$ rather than a description of
one:
\begin{align}
  x_{i,p,t} &= \sum_{a \in \Acal}\ \sum_{\tau \le t}
      s_{i,a,\tau}\;\bar{x}^{\,T_a(P^0_i,\delta_{i,a})}_{i,p,\,t-\tau}
      && \text{(gate: \cref{eq:plan_gate})} \label{eq:chain_gate} \\[3pt]
  d_{i,p,t} &= \mu_{i,p}\,x_{i,p,t}
      && \text{(discharge: \cref{eq:gate_discharge})} \label{eq:chain_flow} \\[3pt]
  Q_{i,p,t+1} &= Q_{i,p,t} + A_{i,p,t} - d_{i,p,t}
      && \text{(conservation: \cref{eq:gate_queue})} \label{eq:chain_queue} \\[3pt]
  D_{i,s,v,t} &\ge \sum_{m \in \Msp_i(s,v)} Q^{w}_{i,m,t}
      && \text{(delay: \cref{eq:mv_waitsum})} \label{eq:chain_delay}
\end{align}
Read as a composition, the plan determines the objective:
\begin{equation}
  Z_1 \;=\; \sum_{t \in \Tsp} c_t
  \;=\; \sum_{t \in \Tsp} \sum_{i \in \Isp} \sum_{p \in \Psp_i}
    \rho_{v(p)}\, F^{D}_{i,p,t}\!\left(
      F^{Q}_{i,p,\cdot}\!\circ F^{d}_{i,p,\cdot}\circ F^{x}_{i,p,\cdot}
      \bigl(P_i\bigr) \right),
  \label{eq:timing_flow_chain}
\end{equation}
where $F^{x}$ is \cref{eq:chain_gate,eq:chain_flow}, $F^{d}$ the
capacity map, $F^{Q}$ the conservation recursion of \cref{eq:chain_queue}, and
$F^{D}$ the waiting sum of \cref{eq:chain_delay}. Three properties of
\cref{eq:timing_flow_chain} are what make the program a schedule program
rather than an accounting one. First, it is a composition of \emph{stated maps},
so $Z_1$ is a function of $P$ alone: there is no route by which a plan changes
$Z_1$ except through its gates, and no coefficient of $P$ is unpriced.
Second, every map is causal in $t$, so unrolling the composition over the horizon
is a forward computation from $Q_{i,p,0}$; a controller that commits $P_i$ can
therefore evaluate $Z_1(P_i)$ before the cycle runs, which is what the solution
methods do. Third, the composition is \emph{exact} rather than fitted: the only
nonlinearity admitted is the integer min of the discharge against available
space, which the program linearises explicitly (\cref{eq:discharge_space,eq:storage_cap}),
so no surrogate coefficient stands between a timing change and its delay.

Consequently the timing is not an input to the delay; it is the delay, and each
arrow of the chain is a deterministic map.  What makes the online
side of this chain \emph{causal} is that every estimator evaluates it on the
\emph{committed} plan $P_i$ -- the global plan-state each junction publishes
on every action and every downstream walker reads (the push of Axiom~2,
Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards}) -- never on the baseline.  A GE at junction $i$
lengthens $P_i$; its gate $x^{\text{main}}_{i,t}$ (\cref{eq:main_open})
stays open longer; the discharge $d_{i,\text{main},t}$ continues; the platoon
leaves later; and by the lagged arrival (\cref{eq:prog_lag}) junction
$j$'s own chain is walked with the delayed arrival -- so the change in timing
at $i$ is visible in the flow at $j$ the moment it is taken, not after the
plan settles.  The four maps of \cref{eq:timing_flow_chain} are the
whole mechanism: they are what ``an action changes the dynamics'' means.

\subsubsection{Green and Cycle Bounds (integer)}
Per-step greens are binary open indicators, so bounds act on cycle-window
sums.  Partition the horizon at $i$ into consecutive cycle windows with step
sets $W_{i,c} \subset \Tsp$, $c = 1,\dots,\lceil T/C_i \rceil$
($|W_{i,c}| = C_i$ except possibly the truncated last window; the roman $W$
always denotes a step set, never the switch indicators $w^{\text{on/off}}$
or the section weights $w_s$):
\begin{align}
  G_i^{\min} \le \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le G_i^{\max}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; c = 1,\dots,\lceil T/C_i \rceil \label{eq:g_bounds} \\[3pt]
  C_i^{\min} - L_i \le \sum_{p \in \Psp_i}\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le C_i^{\max} - L_i
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; c = 1,\dots,\lceil T/C_i \rceil \label{eq:c_bounds}
\end{align}
When $C_i$ is fixed ($C_i^{\min} = C_i^{\max} = C_i$, as in the 135\,s scored
runs), the cycle sum is conserved: GR moves open steps within
$W_{i,c}$ while GE/PI/ER change the sum only through
\cref{eq:couple_ge,eq:couple_gr2} subject to these bounds.

\subsubsection{Delay Counting}
Delay is counted where it happens: queued vehicles waiting behind a shut
gate, summed over integer steps.  The forcing counts below stay, but only
as inputs to arrival propagation (\cref{eq:arr_prop}); delay itself
needs no forcing model.  With $\Delta t = 1$ the forcing in effect at
integer step $t$ is counted per letter, covering the full alphabet
$\Ksp = \Acal = \{\text{GE},\text{INS},\text{SKIP},\text{OFFSET},\text{ER},\text{GR},\text{VP},\text{PT},\text{OC},\text{NA}\}$.
Rather than enumerate the alphabet, partition it once by \emph{what the letter
forces} and let the counts fall out as sums over that partition:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  \Acal^{\mathrm{op}} &= \bigl\{a \in \Acal \;:\; a \text{ forces an open bus step}\bigr\}
    = \{\text{GE},\text{PI}\}, \\
  \Acal^{\mathrm{cl}} &= \bigl\{a \in \Acal \;:\; a \text{ forces early closure of a bus step}\bigr\}
    = \{\text{ER}\},
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:eff_sets}
\end{equation}
(\text{PI} denotes phase insertion.)  Every remaining letter
($\text{GR}, \text{VP}, \text{PT}, \text{OC}, \text{SKIP}, \text{OFFSET}, \text{NA}$) belongs to neither set
and so contributes no forcing term, by construction rather than by a written
zero.  The effective forcing counts are therefore
\begin{align}
  \delta_{i,\text{main},t}^{\text{eff}}
    &= \sum_{a \in \Acal^{\mathrm{op}}} y_{i,a,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp, \label{eq:eff_main} \\
  \delta_{i,\text{side},t}^{\text{eff}}
    &= \sum_{a \in \Acal^{\mathrm{op}} \cup \Acal^{\mathrm{cl}}} y_{i,a,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:eff_side}
\end{align}
all binary sums, hence integers in $\{0,1,2\}$ (main) and $\{0,\dots,3\}$
(side).  GE and INS force bus-green-open steps; ER forces early closure of
cross gates.  GR contributes zero forcing steps by construction: it moves
open steps within the same cycle window (\cref{eq:couple_gr,eq:couple_gr2}
preserve the cycle sum), so there is no net red to price; NA contributes
zero by definition.  VP, PT, OC, SKIP, and OFFSET contribute zero \emph{net} forcing in
this sum by the same argument as GR -- they change \emph{when} gates open
(skip/rotate sequence, shift the offset), not how much bus-green exists in
the cycle -- so their delay effect enters through the plan-to-gate map
(\cref{eq:plan_gate}) and the queue recursion, not through these
per-step coefficients; nothing is missing, every letter the exact program
admits is priced.

\paragraph{Delay is counted per movement, not per phase.}
Delay is not a property of a phase: a phase is a \emph{set} of compatible
movements (Sec.~\ref{sec:dvar_indexing}) and a signal opens all of them at
once, so the waiting that matters happens at the movement whose queue is not
being served.  We therefore make the \emph{movement} the unit of storage,
discharge and waiting, and let the phase enter only through the single
openness flag $x_{i,p(m),t}$ that releases it.  Concretely, a movement is
$m = (\text{approach } a,\ \text{lane-group } g,\ \text{turn class } c)$: each
approach's lanes partition into lane-groups, each lane-group $g$ carries a
movement set $M_g$ with its own storage $K_{i,g}$ and its own per-lane-group
discharge $\mu_{i,g} = \sum_{\ell \in \Lsp_{i,g}} \mu_{i,\ell}$.  With
$A_{i,m,t}$ the arrivals on movement $m$ (aggregating to
$A_{i,p,t} = \sum_{m:\,\Psp_i(m) = p} A_{i,m,t}$, \cref{eq:mu_lanes}):
{\small
\begin{align}
  Q_{i,m,t+1} &= Q_{i,m,t} + A_{i,m,t} - d_{i,m,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; m \in \Msp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:mv_q} \\[3pt]
  d_{i,m,t} &\le \mu_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t},\qquad
    d_{i,m,t} \le Q_{i,m,t} + A_{i,m,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; m \in \Msp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:mv_dcap} \\[3pt]
  Q^w_{i,m,t} &\le Q_{i,m,t},\quad
  Q^w_{i,m,t} &\le K_{i,m}\,(1-x_{i,p(m),t}),\quad
  Q^w_{i,m,t} &\ge Q_{i,m,t} - K_{i,m}\,x_{i,p(m),t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; m \in \Msp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:mv_wait} \\[3pt]
  D_{i,s,v,t} &\ge \sum_{m \in \Msp_i(s,v)} Q^w_{i,m,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; s \in \Ssp,\; v \in \Vsp,\; t \in \Tsp
    \label{eq:mv_waitsum}
\end{align}}
A shut phase ($x_{i,p(m),t} = 0$) forces $Q^w_{i,m,t} = Q_{i,m,t}$: every queued
vehicle on that movement waits one step.  An open phase forces
$Q^w_{i,m,t} = 0$: discharges pass without waiting \emph{this} step, their
earlier waiting already counted while the gate was shut.

\paragraph{Physical spillback: finite link storage.}
A queue cannot exceed the street it sits on, and an open gate cannot discharge
into space that is not there.  Each directed link $(i,j) \in \Esp$ has integer
storage $K_{ij}$ (veh); the two constraints that couple storage to the flow
block are the bound on the queue at the link's head,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:storage_cap}
  Q_{j,t} \le K_{ij} \qquad \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp,
\end{equation}
and, for phases $p \in \Psp_{i\to j}$ at $i$ whose movements enter link
$(i,j)$, the discharge-against-space bound
\begin{equation}\label{eq:discharge_space}
  d_{i,p,t} \le K_{ij} - Q_{j,t} + M^{\text{big}}\,(1 - x_{i,p,t})
  \qquad \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; p \in \Psp_{i\to j},\; t \in \Tsp,
\end{equation}
so an open gate discharges at most the free storage downstream (a shut gate
discharges nothing, already forced by $d \le \mu g$ with $g = x$; the big-$M$
term only relaxes the bound there).  Without these two constraints released
platoons would vanish into full links and corridor performance would be
overstated: they are what make downstream gridlock physically felt upstream.
The big-$M$ term is the \emph{only} linearisation in the flow block; its R4
online counterpart sits strictly inside it ($Q^{\text{jam}}_{j} \le K_{ij}$), so
the rule trips before the street does.

\paragraph{Delay accrues only while vehicles are actually queued.}
The waiting quantity $Q^w_{i,m,t}$ is the number of vehicles \emph{in the standing
queue} on movement $m$ at step $t$ that are blocked that step, so the delay it
generates is \emph{queueing} delay: it is incurred only by a vehicle that is
held, and only on the steps it is held. A vehicle that arrives and discharges
within the same step, or one already discharging, is never in $Q^w$ and is
never charged. This matters for how $D$ should be read: $D_{i,s,v,t}$ is
\emph{not} a per-vehicle travel time, and not a free-flow or
time-loss-while-moving term. It is the accumulated held-vehicle time of
section--class $(s,v)$, which is what a signal-control program can be held
accountable for, since \emph{everything the program controls is a gate}. Travel
time that no gate caused --- a vehicle's own free-flow traversal, or delay
incurred downstream of $i$ --- is outside $D$ and is handled by the corridor
capacities of \cref{sec:coordination} rather than double-counted here.
With $\Msp_i(s,v)$ the movements of approach class $s$ and class $v$, and
inverse class map $v(m)$ used in \cref{eq:stage_cost}, the section--class
delay at $(i,s,v,t)$ is the movement waiting summed over its movements. This is
the \emph{sole} delay definition in the program: $D$ is lower-bounded once, at
the finest granularity the network distinguishes, and it is tight at optimum since
$Z_1$ minimises $D$. Exact at every attainable point by construction: no fitted
coefficients, no approximation --- and, being a counting identity over a queue
that the program itself creates and clears, it can never charge a vehicle for
time it did not spend stopped at a signal.

\paragraph{Lane groups: where a shared lane blocks its own approach.}
A movement is not an independent server.  Lanes are pooled into
lane-groups $g$, and a group discharges only what its lanes can carry
\emph{while open under a phase that serves it}, so a full turn bay stops the
through movement sharing its lane.  We state that coupling explicitly, because
without it the per-movement queues above would each look feasible while the
shared lane is oversaturated.  Let $G_{i,g} = \sum_{m \in M_g} Q_{i,m,t}$ be the
group occupancy, $x^{\cup}_{i,g,t} = \max_{m \in M_g} x_{i,p(m),t}$ its union
green (a constant-times-binary product, linearised below), and $K_{i,g}$ the
group's storage:
\begin{align}
  G_{i,g,t} &\le K_{i,g}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; g \in \Gsp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:lg_storage} \\[3pt]
  x^{\cup}_{i,g,t} &\le \sum_{m \in M_g} x_{i,p(m),t},\quad
  x^{\cup}_{i,g,t} \ge x_{i,p(m),t} \;\forall m \in M_g
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; g \in \Gsp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:lg_union} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{m \in M_g} d_{i,m,t} &\le \mu_{i,g}\; x^{\cup}_{i,g,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; g \in \Gsp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:lg_release} \\[3pt]
  G_{i,g,t} &\ge \sum_{m \in M_g} Q_{i,m,t} - \Bigl(\sum_{m \in M_g}
      Q_{i,m,t} - \min_{m \in M_g} Q_{i,m,t}\Bigr)\bigl(1 - x^{\cup}_{i,g,t}\bigr)
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; g \in \Gsp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:lg_block}
\end{align}
\cref{eq:lg_storage} is the physical limit: a lane group holds at
most its lanes.  \cref{eq:lg_union} defines the union green as
exactly ``some phase serving this group is open'', which is what a shared lane
physically sees.  \cref{eq:lg_release} then caps the group's
discharge by its own saturation under that union green, so a group cannot
discharge more than its lanes carry even when several of its movements are
open at once.  \cref{eq:lg_block} is the within-group blocking
constraint: a group that is \emph{shut} cannot be emptied, so any movement
queue in excess of the group's minimum cannot leave, and a full turn bay
therefore blocks the through movement that shares its lane exactly as a full
street does.  Every quantity stays integer-linear: $\mu_{i,g}$ and
$K_{i,g}$ are integer constants, the $x^{\cup}$ products are constant-times-
binary (the same McCormick argument as \cref{eq:pog_loss} applies
unchanged), and \cref{eq:lg_block} is a linear cut on integer $Q$.

The link-level special case is recovered rather than contradicted: taking each
lane-group to be a whole approach and $\Msp_i(s,v)$ a singleton recovers the
one-server-per-section model, and taking $\Ksp$ to the per-movement $K_{i,m}$
recovers per-movement storage without lane sharing.  The online controllers
already read this view (phase $\to$ turnings $\to$ origin lanes, the
$[$\texttt{PHASEMAP}$]$ capture), so the movement-level cost is the same
object on both sides of the paper.

\subsubsection{Corridor Coordination}
\label{sec:coordination}

\paragraph{Offset propagation (integer, modulo cycle).}
Each forced open step shifts the cycle clock by one integer step: GE and PI
push subsequent phases later ($+1$ per active step), ER pulls them earlier
($-1$ per active step), GR and NA leave the clock unchanged ($0$).  With
integer wrap auxiliary $z_{i,t} \in \Z$ ($z$ appears only here, never to be
confused with the takeover flag $\chi$ or spill flag $\zeta$ of
Sec.~\ref{sec:rule_tsp}):
\begin{align}
  \phi_{i,t+1} &= \phi_{i,t} + y_{i,\text{GE},t} + y_{i,\text{PI},t} - y_{i,\text{ER},t} - C_i\, z_{i,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \setminus \{T\} \label{eq:offset_state} \\[3pt]
  \phi_{i,0} &= \phi_i^{\text{init}} && \forall i \in \Isp \label{eq:offset_init}
\end{align}
with $\phi_{i,t} \in \{0,\dots,C_i{-}1\}$ enforced by the domain.  Variable-phase
skipping and phase rotation are not part of the exact program: they are online
search extensions (Secs.~\ref{sec:phase_skip}--\ref{sec:phase_rot}) whose
queue/consecutive-skip guards live in
\cref{sec:online_guards}.  The coefficients above propagate to
downstream intersections via the progression constraints, keeping the green
wave intact; without this coupling each intersection would be independent.

\paragraph{Phase-aware offset refinement (integer).}
\cref{eq:offset_state} carries a single scalar coefficient: every forced step
displaces the cycle clock by one, whichever phase is open.  That overstates the
coupling, because an action on the coordinated phase $p_b^i$ moves the
\emph{end} of its green, not its \emph{start}---so the offset, defined as the
main-phase start, is unchanged.  Only actions on phases that \emph{precede}
$p_b^i$ in the cycle displace it.  We therefore (i) extend the action index to
carry the phase it modifies, $y_{i,p,k,t}$, (ii) add a phase selector
$o_{i,p,t}$ naming the targeted phase, and (iii) weight the offset recurrence by
the precedence of that phase:
\begin{align}
  a_{i,p} &= \mathbf{1}\!\left[\,p \prec_i p_b^i\,\right]
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i \label{eq:prec} \\[3pt]
  A_{i,t} &= \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} a_{i,p}\bigl(
        y_{i,p,\text{GE},t} + y_{i,p,\text{PI},t} - y_{i,p,\text{ER},t}\bigr)
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:impulse} \\[3pt]
  \sigma_{i,t+1} &= \sigma_{i,t} + A_{i,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \setminus \{T\} \label{eq:offset_state_p} \\[3pt]
  \sigma_{i,0} &= \sigma_i^{\text{init}}
    && \forall i \in \Isp \label{eq:offset_init_lin}
\end{align}
with $a_{i,p_b^i}=0$ by construction, so a coordinated-phase action contributes
nothing to the main-phase start.  The clock is carried \emph{unwrapped}: $\sigma$
is the absolute start time of the coordinated phase, a plain integer, so no
modular term and no wrap auxiliary appear.  An offset is a difference, so only
$\sigma_j-\sigma_i$ is ever constrained, and per-intersection cycle lengths never
enter---no $\gcd(C_i,C_j)$ quotient is needed.  One reference is anchored,
$\sigma_{1,t}=\sigma^{\text{init}}_1$, to fix the gauge.  With the clock unwrapped
the inter-intersection bound is a plain difference, and it is this constraint
that couples an action at one intersection to the timing at its neighbours:
\begin{equation}
  \Delta\phi^{\min}_{ij} \;\le\; \sigma_{j,t} - \sigma_{i,t} \;\le\; \Delta\phi^{\max}_{ij}
  \qquad \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:offset_band_lin}
\end{equation}
Subtracting \cref{eq:offset_state_p} for $i$ from that for $j$ gives
$(\sigma_j-\sigma_i)_{t+1}=(\sigma_j-\sigma_i)_t + A_{j,t} - A_{i,t}$: an action
at $i$ moves the relative offset by $-A_{i,t}$ and one at $j$ by $+A_{j,t}$, so
\cref{eq:offset_band_lin} bounds the actions themselves rather than penalising
them, and holds at every step instead of in aggregate.
Here $a_{i,p_b^i}=0$ by construction, so a coordinated-phase action contributes
nothing to the main-phase start.  The program chooses the phase jointly with the
action; the online controller of Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards} is the tractable
restriction of this exact form, in which the phase is fixed by the currently-open
phase rather than optimised.  The structural distinction is
preserved either way---a coordinated-phase action cannot displace the
main-phase start, whereas one on a preceding phase can---which is what makes the
offset a decision-coupled quantity rather than a fixed plan parameter.  The selector is constrained so the action set stays a genuine
choice:
\begin{align}
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} o_{i,p,t} &= 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:sel_one} \\[3pt]
  y_{i,p,k,t} &\le o_{i,p,t}
    && \forall i,p,k,t \label{eq:sel_gate} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i}\sum_{k \in \Ksp} y_{i,p,k,t} &= 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:excl_p} \\[3pt]
  o_{i,p,t} &\le x_{i,p,t} + \iota_{i,p}
    && \forall i,p,t \label{eq:sel_open} \\[3pt]
  y_{i,p,k,t} &\le x_{i,p,t}
    && \forall i,p,\; k \in \{\text{GE},\text{ER}\},\; t \label{eq:act_open}
\end{align}
where $\iota_{i,p} \in \{0,1\}$ relaxes \cref{eq:sel_open} for the pre-arm
pairs the controller may apply to a closed phase; for all other pairs the
selector can only name a phase that is open.  \cref{eq:act_open} is what makes
the \emph{current} phase enter the program without a nonlinearity: because
exactly one phase is open at each step, a green extension or early release is
forced onto that phase by a linear inequality rather than by a product
$x_{i,p,t}\,y_{i,k,t}$.  Insertion and offset correction are exempt---they target
the coordinated phase regardless of which phase is open---and are handled by the
relaxation $\iota_{i,p}$ instead.

\paragraph{Simultaneous main green and the offset band.}
An offset band alone is not the object of interest: what must be preserved is
that the main phases at adjacent intersections are green \emph{at the same time}.
We therefore introduce the conjunction indicator
\begin{align}
  g_{ij,t} &\le x_{i,p_b^i,t} && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:g_up} \\[3pt]
  g_{ij,t} &\le x_{j,p_b^j,t} && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:g_dn} \\[3pt]
  g_{ij,t} &\ge x_{i,p_b^i,t} + x_{j,p_b^j,t} - 1
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:g_and}
\end{align}
which is exact: $g_{ij,t}=1$ iff the upstream main phase at $i$ and the
downstream main phase at $j$ are both open.  Requiring the overlap to persist
for a minimum band replaces the symmetric tolerance of \cref{eq:prog_tol} by
the constraint that actually matters,
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} g_{ij,t} \;\ge\; B^{\min}_{ij}
  \qquad \forall (i,j) \in \Esp ,
  \label{eq:band_min}
\end{equation}
and it binds the actions only through the phases that can move the offset: a
curtailment on a preceding phase shifts $\phi_i$, sliding the main-phase window
and shrinking $\sum_t g_{ij,t}$, whereas the same curtailment on $p_b^i$ leaves
the window in place.  The bound $B^{\min}_{ij}$ thus plays the role of
$\delta^{\text{prog}}$ without asserting a fixed band around the free-flow lag,
and \cref{eq:prog_tol} is retained only as the monitor that triggers
re-optimisation when the realised overlap approaches the bound.

\paragraph{Progression constraints (integer).}
With binary split selector $\beta_{ij,t}\in\{0,1\}$ holding the decomposition
complementary (otherwise $+\!1$ on both sides would fake extra mismatch):
\begin{align}
  \phi_{j,t} - \phi_{i,t} - \ell_{ij} &= \epsilon_{ij,t}^+ - \epsilon_{ij,t}^-
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:prog} \\[3pt]
  \epsilon_{ij,t}^+,\;\epsilon_{ij,t}^- &\in \Z_{\ge 0} && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:eps_split} \\[3pt]
  \epsilon_{ij,t}^+ + \epsilon_{ij,t}^- &\le \delta^{\text{prog}} && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:prog_tol} \\[3pt]
  \epsilon_{ij,t}^+ &\le M^{\text{big}}\beta_{ij,t},\quad
  \epsilon_{ij,t}^- \le M^{\text{big}}(1-\beta_{ij,t})
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:eps_comp}
\end{align}
with integer lag $\ell_{ij} = \tau_{ij}$ (since $\Delta t = 1$).  Bandwidth
$B_{ij,t} = x_{j,p_b^j,t} - \epsilon_{ij,t}^+ - \epsilon_{ij,t}^-$ is an integer
expression for the $Z_2$ monitor, not a decision variable.

\paragraph{Two-way offsets (bidirectional bandwidth).}
One-way progression above serves the timetabled direction.  To design the
stagger for both directions instead (MAXBAND-style
\citep{Little1981MAXBAND:Networks,Gartner1991AMULTIBAND}), mirror the mismatch
machinery inbound with symmetric lags $\ell_{ji}$:
\begin{align}
  \phi_{i,t} - \phi_{j,t} - \ell_{ji} &= \eta_{ij,t}^+ - \eta_{ij,t}^-
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:prog_in} \\[3pt]
  \eta_{ij,t}^+ + \eta_{ij,t}^- &\le \delta^{\text{prog}} && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:prog_in_tol} \\[3pt]
  \eta_{ij,t}^+ &\le M^{\text{big}}\beta^{\text{in}}_{ij,t},\quad
  \eta_{ij,t}^- \le M^{\text{big}}(1-\beta^{\text{in}}_{ij,t})
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:prog_in_comp}
\end{align}
Directional through-bands $b^+_t, b^-_t \in \Z_{\ge 0}$ (bandwidths; the bare
$b \in \Bsp$ remains the bus index) are bounded by worst-link green minus
mismatch and floored by design parameters $B^{+,\min}, B^{-,\min}$:
\begin{align}
  b^+_t &\le G^b_i - (\epsilon_{ij,t}^+ + \epsilon_{ij,t}^-)
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:band_out} \\[3pt]
  b^-_t &\le G^b_i - (\eta_{ij,t}^+ + \eta_{ij,t}^-)
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:band_in} \\[3pt]
  b^+_t &\ge B^{+,\min},\quad b^-_t \ge B^{-,\min}
    && \forall t \in \Tsp \label{eq:band_floor}
\end{align}
with $G^b_i$ the bus-phase green steps at $i$.  Setting $B^{-,\min} = 0$
recovers the one-way program exactly.  Both floors are jointly feasible only
when the directional offset demands agree modulo $C$ within tolerance;
otherwise the program correctly reports infeasibility, and the trade-off
belongs in the objective (weighted MAXBAND form) rather than the
constraints.

\paragraph{Repayable, lagged offset state: the full action-to-band chain.}
\cref{eq:offset_state} is the whole clock coupling, and it has two
defects worth stating plainly, because both are visible in the runs of
Sec.~\ref{sec:results}.  First, it has no repayment term, so a single
cycle-extending letter shifts $\phi$ permanently for the rest of the horizon
-- the program faithfully predicts what the code does (measured: recovery trims
never fire on a saturated corridor, so every extension drifts), but it cannot
express the recovery mechanism the code attempts, nor prefer a grant that is
repayable over one that is not.  Second, the mismatch
\cref{eq:prog} compares $\phi$ at the same $t$ on both links, so a shift at
$i$ is felt at $j$ instantly rather than after the travel lag $\ell_{ij}$ --
the temporal half of the spatial lag is missing.  The following closes both,
and makes the action-to-offset-to-bandwidth coupling explicit.

Let $s^{\text{in}}_{i,t} = y_{i,\text{GE},t} + y_{i,\text{PI},t}$ and
$s^{\text{out}}_{i,t} = y_{i,\text{ER},t}$ (forward- and backward-shifting
letters; GR/OC/NA carry $0$, as before), let $w^{\text{trim}}_{i,t} \in
\Z_{\ge 0}$ be cross-phase steps trimmed this step to repay debt, and let
$d_{i,t} \in \Z_{\ge 0}$ be the outstanding cycle-length debt.  Then
\begin{align}
  \phi_{i,t+1} &= \phi_{i,t} + s^{\text{in}}_{i,t} - s^{\text{out}}_{i,t}
    - w^{\text{trim}}_{i,t} - C_i\, z_{i,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \setminus \{T\} \label{eq:offset_repay} \\[3pt]
  d_{i,t+1} &= d_{i,t} + s^{\text{in}}_{i,t} - s^{\text{out}}_{i,t}
    - w^{\text{trim}}_{i,t} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \setminus \{T\}
    \label{eq:debt_state} \\[3pt]
  d_{i,0} &= 0 && \forall i \in \Isp \label{eq:debt_init}
\end{align}
so the clock shift and the debt are the same quantity accounted twice: what a
letter adds, a trim removes, and the offset never drifts by more than the debt
outstanding.  Trims are drawn only from trimmable cross slack, and never below
minimum green:
\begin{align}
  w^{\text{trim}}_{i,t} &\le
    \sum_{p \in \Psp_i \setminus \{p_b^i\}}
      \Bigl(\sum_{t' \in W_{i,c}}\,\bar{x}_{i,p,t'} - G_i^{\min}\Bigr)^{\!}
      \cdot \mathbf{1}[t \in W_{i,c}]
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:trim_budget} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} x_{i,p,t} &\ge \bar{x}_{i,p,t} - w^{\text{trim}}_{i,t}
    \qquad\text{(at most one step per trim, summed over $p$)}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:trim_legality}
\end{align}
\cref{eq:trim_budget} is the repayable budget $\mathcal{H}^{\text{rep}}_{i,t}$,
the seconds of cross green above minimum green in the current cycle window;
\cref{eq:trim_legality} keeps every trim a legal (above-minimum) phase
reduction, so trimming cannot manufacture a red out of a protected stage.
Repayment is then a windowed accounting, with $K$ windows the same
single-parameter horizon as the code (\texttt{RECOVERY\_MAX\_CYCLES}):
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{c' = c}^{c + K - 1}\ \sum_{t \in W_{i,c'}} w^{\text{trim}}_{i,t}
  \;\ge\; \sum_{c' = c}^{c + K - 1}\ \sum_{t \in W_{i,c'}}
    \bigl(s^{\text{in}}_{i,t} - s^{\text{out}}_{i,t}\bigr)
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; c
  \label{eq:repay_clear}
\end{equation}
and, crucially, an action whose shift exceeds what the junction can ever repay
is \emph{inadmissible} rather than merely expensive:
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} s^{\text{in}}_{i,t}
  \;\le\; \mathcal{H}^{\text{rep}}_{i,t}
    + M^{\text{big}}\Bigl(1 - \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} y_{i,k,t}\Bigr)
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:rule_repay}
\end{equation}
\cref{eq:rule_repay} is the exact-program twin of the code's
recoverability gate and of the solver clamp that bounds a magnitude by
recoverable headroom: a junction holding $\mathcal{H}^{\text{rep}} = 0$ admits
no cycle-extending letter at all, which is the behaviour that produces
saturated-corridor non-acting rather than silent permanent drift.

Finally the lag.  The displacement a letter at $i$ inflicts on the downstream
green is felt only after the platoon travels, so the mismatch variables are
defined against the lagged clock:
\begin{equation}
  \phi_{j,t} - \phi_{i,t-\ell_{ij}} = \epsilon_{ij,t}^+ - \epsilon_{ij,t}^-
  \qquad \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:prog_lag}
\end{equation}
replacing the same-time comparison in \cref{eq:prog} (with
$\phi_{i,t-\ell} := \phi_i^{\text{init}}$ for early $t$); the inbound mirror is
lagged identically.  With these four additions the coupling chain is closed and
each link is a real mechanism rather than a shared index: a grant at $(i,t)$
adds open bus steps (\cref{eq:couple_ge,eq:couple_pi}) $\Rightarrow$
the cycle sum moves (\cref{eq:c_bounds}) $\Rightarrow$ $s^{\text{in}}$ raises
$\phi$ and $d$ (\cref{eq:offset_repay,eq:debt_state}) $\Rightarrow$ the
lagged mismatch at $(i,j)$ grows (\cref{eq:prog_lag}) $\Rightarrow$ the
directional bandwidths shrink (\cref{eq:band_out,eq:band_in}) and the
Purdue loss rises (\cref{eq:pog_loss}); the debt is then discharged by
trims (\cref{eq:trim_budget,eq:repay_clear}) if, and only if, the
junction had the headroom to grant (\cref{eq:rule_repay}).  GR and OC leave
$\phi$ untouched, which is exact for the bus clock and for the cycle total but
\emph{not} for the cross clock: they move side-phase starts, so the inbound
band \cref{eq:band_in} must read the side green as starting at
$\phi_{i,t} + G^b_i$ (side phase follows the bus phase in the two-phase order) rather than at
$\phi_{i,t}$; a one-line correction we state here rather than silently assume.
A deliberate offset correction has no variable in the program, so OC passes
\cref{eq:rule_repay} and \cref{eq:rule_prog} by construction, as
\cref{eq:offset_state} already records.

\paragraph{Local recursion, network-aware decision.}
\cref{eq:offset_state} is local by physics--each controller owns only its
own clock, so the \emph{transition} of $\phi_{i,t}$ touches no other
intersection's state.  The \emph{values} of the offsets are nevertheless
corridor-joint, through four channels that force every $\phi_{i,t}$ to
account for the rest of the corridor: (i) the progression equalities
(\cref{eq:prog}) tie each offset directly to its neighbours';
(ii) the shared objective $Z_1$ prices every intersection's queue in one sum,
so shifting $i$'s clock is weighed against $j$'s delay; (iii) the spillback
rule (\cref{eq:rule_spill_jam,eq:rule_spill_block}) blocks $i$'s
releases on $j$'s predicted queue state $\zeta_{ij,t}$; and (iv) the bus
clocks $\tau_{b,i}$ propagate upstream actions downstream
(\cref{eq:arr_prop}), with the coupling below closing the loop back onto
the gates.  The input profile $A$ is likewise assumed corridor-consistent:
downstream arrivals equal the corresponding upstream discharges plus
exogenous demand, so neighbour states enter through the data as well as the
equations.

\paragraph{Bus-progression coupling.}
The mechanism is direction-agnostic but free progression is not: offsets,
progression equalities, and the coupling below apply to every bus along its
own route direction, so opposing buses are constrained identically
(\cref{fig:sched} shows one bus each way).  One stagger cannot serve
both directions at once, however: the $+10$\,s offsets that carry the
timetabled bus deliver a red wave to the return trip, whose unserved waits are the
gray counterfactual segments in the figure.  The opposing bus is therefore served by
priority under the same guards, caps, and spillback protection, not by free
greens.  A green wave that ignores the bus it serves fails
its purpose, so the downstream
gate must be open when the propagated bus clock says the bus gets there.
The precise form of that requirement lives with the arrival spine, not here:
\cref{eq:prog_bus} in Sec.~\ref{sec:arrival_spine} forces the bus gate open
\emph{at} the assigned arrival step (not merely once in a preceding window ---
a window form would be implied by any fixed cycle and bind nowhere, constraining
neither the timetable, which is enforced by \cref{eq:sched_cap}, nor the
objective, which already prices every shut-gate wait through $Z_1$).  Because
$\tau_{b,j}$ (hence $e_{b,j,t}$) carries the accumulated effect of every
upstream forcing decision via \cref{eq:arr_prop}, that constraint makes
$j$'s operation an explicit function of $i$'s state for all $i$ upstream of
$j$--the offset at $j$ cannot be set without considering the corridor.  It
belongs to the plant set, so every exact optimum respects it; the online
code only approximates it, greedily, through the downstream chain and cascade
costs of Sections~\ref{sec:gate_cascade} and \ref{sec:online_guards}.

\paragraph{Re-optimization: correcting toward a new optimum.}
Because the offsets $\phi_{i,t}$ are decision variables--not inputs--every
solve outputs \emph{optimal offsets} $\phi^*_{i,t}$ jointly with the gates
and clocks.  When reality drifts from the plan (a platoon arrives late, a
spillback appears, an online grant deviates), the old $\phi^*$ is stale, but
a new optimum is always available by re-solving from where the corridor
actually is.  At any integer correction instant $t_c$, fix the measured state
$\hat{X}_{t_c}$ (queues, clocks, bus positions) as the new initial condition
and re-solve the same program over the window
$\Tsp(t_c) = \{t_c,\dots,\min(t_c + H^{\text{corr}},\,T)\}$ ($H$ family:
horizons):
\begin{equation}
  \text{\cref{eq:opt_crit} re-posed on $\Tsp(t_c)$ with
    $X_{t_c}=\hat{X}_{t_c}$.}
  \label{eq:reopt}
\end{equation}
with $Q_{i,p,t_c}$, $\phi_{i,t_c}$, $\tau_{b,i}$ initialised from measurement
rather than prediction.  The \emph{offset correction} is then simply the
difference $\phi^*_{i,t} - \phi^{\text{old}}_{i,t}$ for $t \ge t_c$: the
prescribed clock shift that steers the corridor from the disturbed state
back onto a jointly optimal progression.  The mismatch variables
$\epsilon^{\pm}_{ij,t}$ already meter the need for correction online--when
$|\epsilon_{ij,t}|$ approaches $\delta^{\text{prog}}$, the plan is at the
edge of its green-wave budget and \cref{eq:reopt} should be re-triggered.
The all--no-action continuation from $t_c$ is always feasible, so a
correction always exists.  Offset-correction ($\text{OC}$) actions (solved signed shifts, fired at $\ge3$\,s and bounded above by remaining-red minus min-green; $\pm$1--10\,s observed on these corridors, not a fixed grid) are the greedy single-step truncation of this
exact recourse: shift-now versus re-plan-the-window.

\label{sec:offset_recourse}
\paragraph{The offset half is solvable in closed form; the split half is not
warranted.} The exact recourse above has two separable parts.  Moving
$\omega_i$, the bus-green \emph{start}, is a one-dimensional circular problem
with an exact answer: the cheapest correction from the current start
$\bar{s}_i$ to a target $s_i^{\star}$ is the shortest signed wrap
\begin{equation}
  \Delta_i^{\text{off}} = \bigl( ( s_i^{\star} - \bar{s}_i + C_i/2 ) \bmod C_i \bigr) - C_i/2 ,
  \label{eq:off_push}
\end{equation}
subject to a floor $|\Delta_i^{\text{off}}| \ge \delta^{\text{off}}_{\min}$ and a
ceiling $|\Delta_i^{\text{off}}| \le \delta^{\text{off}}_{\max}$ that the
remainder re-aims over subsequent cycles.  Redrawing the \emph{splits} has no
such structure: it trades one saturation flow against another and was measured,
not merely suspected, to be harmful.  An A/B of automated Webster shares against
the hand-tuned greens cost $-46\%$ objective on one platform and $5$--$7\%$
throughput on the other, because the shares starve an already main-saturated
corridor.  The base plan is therefore held \emph{given}, and
$\Delta_i^{\text{off}}$ of
\cref{eq:off_push} is admitted as a guarded plan-level letter
(\texttt{OPP}), offered alongside \texttt{OC} rather than instead of it:
$\texttt{OC}$ chases one bus's arrival, \texttt{OPP} chases the platoon's.  The
targets $s_i^{\star}$ come from a joint stage-order/offset solve --- an exact
Viterbi dynamic program minimising two-directional arrival-in-green miss, in
which a bus departs its upstream junction mid-green --- not from a fixed-speed
travel-time cumulant.  On Logan that solve reduces mean time-outside-green from
$7.78$\,s to $0.70$\,s ($-91\%$; $-83\%$ averaged over corridor speeds
$10$--$14$\,m/s), which is the quantity the dynamic program minimises.  Two
caveats are carried forward rather than smoothed over: the improvement is a
\emph{progression} quantity and does not follow for an arbitrary fixed-entry
arrival (a departures-in-green proxy falls $43.5\%\to40.2\%$), and
\texttt{OPP} is default-off and absent from the reported runs, so it enters
$\Ksp$ only once its arm has results.

\subsubsection{Bus Arrival Times and Schedule Adherence (integer)}
\label{sec:arrival_spine}

\paragraph{The spine, stated first.}
The program minimises one quantity: total passenger delay,
$Z_1 = \sum_t c_t$ (\cref{eq:stage_cost}).  Every quantity introduced from
here on is a term in that single sum, and none of them is a target in its own
right.  What does need naming before any constraint is written is \emph{where} the
bus-related part of that delay enters, because the per-bus clocks are how it is
expressed, bounded, and reported.  Upstream and downstream contributions do
compete --- holding vehicles for a bus holds vehicles that were otherwise moving,
and relieving an upstream approach speeds the buses queued behind it --- but that
competition is settled by the total-delay objective together with the lateness
and corridor-travel-time caps of \cref{sec:objectives}, never by
privileging either clock.  Fix a bus $b$ on route
$\Rcal_b = (i_0,\dots,i_N)$ and let $\bar{\tau}_{b,i}$ be its timetabled
passage time at $i$.  Then the two clocks are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  \underbrace{\tau_{b,i}}_{\substack{\text{actual passage}\\\text{step at } i}}
    &\quad \text{and}\quad
  \underbrace{\sigma_{b,i} = \tau_{b,i} - \bar{\tau}_{b,i}}_{\substack{\text{schedule}\\\text{deviation}}}
    \in \bigl[\, -\infty,\; \epsilon^{\text{late}} \,\bigr],
  \label{eq:spine}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
for every $i \in \Rcal_b$.  Two structural facts organise the rest of the
program, and we flag them here so the reader can orient:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{The arrival clock is induced by the signal settings.} The
        decisions are the signal settings themselves --- which gates are open at
        $(i,t)$. The arrival time $\tau_{b,i}$ is not itself chosen; it is what
        the timetable becomes once it has been propagated through those gates
        (\cref{eq:timing_flow_chain}), so a plan is judged by the clock it
        induces at every downstream intersection. $\tau_{b,i}$ appears as a
        variable only because we report it and bound it
        (\cref{eq:sched_cap}); nothing sets it directly. This is why the
        program is a schedule and not a policy: it commits signal settings, and
        the passage times follow from them.
  \item \textbf{Headway is the shadow price of the passage clock.} With
        $\sigma_{b,i}$ bounded above, the constraint
        $\sigma_{b,i} \le \epsilon^{\text{late}}$ (\cref{eq:sched_cap})
        is what makes two buses on one route interact at all. Serving $b$
        late does not merely delay $b$; it spends the lateness budget that the
        following bus needs. That budget is spent against total delay: the
        program keeps whichever signal setting leaves the smaller
        $Z_1 = \sum_t c_t$ (\cref{eq:stage_cost}), subject to the lateness cap
        and the corridor-travel-time cap of \cref{sec:objectives}. There
        is no bus term competing against a separate car term --- the delay is
        added in passenger-seconds at occupancies $\rho_{\text{bus}} = 40$ and
        $\rho_{\text{car}} = 1.5$, so the trade is made once, on one scale.
\end{enumerate}

The remaining clauses in this subsubsection \emph{discretise} the two clocks:
they force $\tau$ onto the integer step grid, split $\sigma$ into early and
late parts, and cap the late part.  Nothing later in the program introduces a
third clock.

Each bus $b$ traverses its route $i_0,\dots,i_N$ hop by hop
($h = 0,\dots,N-1$ is the hop index, used only here).  The step
containing its arrival is selected linearly by $e_{b,i,t}$ instead of a
ceiling of a continuous variable:
\begin{align}
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} e_{b,i,t} &= 1 && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:arr_assign} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} t\, e_{b,i,t} &= \tau_{b,i} && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:arr_link} \\[3pt]
  \tau_{b,i_{h+1}} &\ge \tau_{b,i_{h}} + \tau^{\text{ff}}_{i_{h},i_{h+1}}
    + a^{\sigma}_{i_{h},h} + b^{\sigma}_{i_{h},h} \cdot \delta_{i_{h},\text{main},t}^{\text{eff}}
    - M^{\text{big}}\,(1 - e_{b,i_{h},t})
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; h = 0,\dots,N-1,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:arr_prop} \\[3pt]
  \sigma_{b,i} &= \tau_{b,i} - \bar{\tau}_{b,i}
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_def} \\[3pt]
  \sigma_{b,i} &= \sigma_{b,i}^+ - \sigma_{b,i}^-, \qquad
    \sigma_{b,i}^{\pm} \in \Z_{\ge 0}
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_split} \\[3pt]
  \sigma_{b,i}^+ &\le M^{\text{big}}o_{b,i},\quad
  \sigma_{b,i}^- \le M^{\text{big}}(1-o_{b,i}),\quad o_{b,i}\in\{0,1\}
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_comp} \\[3pt]
  \sigma_{b,i}^+ &\le \epsilon^{\text{late}}
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_cap} \\[3pt]
  \tau_{b,i_0} &= \tau_b^{\text{detect}}
    && \forall b \in \Bsp \label{eq:arr_init}
\end{align}
\cref{eq:arr_prop} is the integer time-based coupling that makes this
a scheduling program: the assignment $e_{b,i_h,t} = 1$ selects the integer
step $t = \tau_{b,i_h}$, activates the corresponding effective-red
$\delta^{\text{eff}}_{i_h,\text{main},t}$ through the big-$M$, and shifts
$\tau_{b,i_{h+1}}$ through the integer hyperplane slope--exactly the
mechanism by which forced bus-open steps move bus arrivals across the integer
grid.  \cref{eq:sched_cap} is the on-schedule requirement: when the
timetable is attainable, no bus may
arrive more than $\epsilon^{\text{late}}$ steps behind $\bar{\tau}_{b,i}$ at any
intersection on its route; when it is not (e.g.\ an upstream incident makes
every schedule infeasible), the cap is relaxed and $Z_3$ alone ranks
schedules by aggregate tardiness.  Earliness is left unpenalised so the
program never forces a bus to loiter.  The chain is completed by the
bus-progression coupling: whenever the program assigns bus $b$'s arrival at
$(j,t)$, the bus gate there must be open \emph{at} $t$,
\begin{equation}
  x_{j,p_b^j,t} \;\ge\; e_{b,j,t}
  \qquad \forall b \in \Bsp,\; j \in \Rcal_b,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:prog_bus}
\end{equation}
i.e.\ an assigned arrival is always served on green, never into a shut gate.
This is the load-bearing form: unlike a recency window (``open at least once
in the preceding $H$ steps,'' which any fixed cycle satisfies trivially and
which constrains neither the timetable nor the objective), it forces the plan
to serve what it schedules, so $e_{b,j,t}=1$ commits green rather than merely
predicting arrival.  Together \cref{eq:arr_assign,eq:arr_link},
\ref{eq:arr_prop}, \ref{eq:sched_def}--\ref{eq:sched_cap} and
\ref{eq:prog_bus} are the complete timetable chain: assign, propagate, bound
lateness, serve on green.  Delay for any residual wait still accrues through
$Z_1$ via the queue recursion, so the coupling adds obligation, not cost.

\paragraph{Scope of the program: signal settings only.}
Everything introduced above is a quantity the signal controller can change or a
consequence of such a change over the whole horizon: gate openness
$x_{i,p,t}$, the action and its integer duration, green and cycle bounds, the
offset $\phi_{i,t}$, and the queues and arrival clocks those settings induce.
The program contains no detector, no clock, and no test of the corridor's current
state, because a whole-horizon program is not evaluated \emph{at} a moment. The
following are therefore \emph{absent by construction} and are supplied instead by
the solution algorithm (\cref{sec:online_guards}): bus-detection windows
and decision epochs, the arrivals-on-green tracker and its progression-loss veto,
cooldown and inter-grant timing, spillback and congestion gates, the
conditional-priority rule layer R1--R11, and action arbitration. None of them
appears in any constraint of \cref{eq:excl,eq:arr_init}; in particular
the gate profile of \cref{eq:plan_gate} is a function of the chosen action
alone, with no feasibility screen applied to that action. The exact program and
the deployed controller are consequently two different objects: the first asks
which gate schedule is best given perfect foresight, the second asks which
admissible action to take given estimates. Their shared content is the
street physics, not the decision rule.

\section{Solution Methods}
\label{sec:solutions}
% ==============================================================================

The exact program of \cref{sec:milp} is a whole-horizon,
clairvoyant, integer-time plant model.  The code never solves it jointly:
at each decision epoch $t \in \Tsp^{\text{trig}}$--a bus detection, a timer
tick under \texttt{CONTINUOUS\_MONITOR\_MODE}, or an empty-phase event--a single
junction solves a single-action greedy subproblem from an \emph{estimated}
state $\hat{X}_t$ (measured flows/queues, Kalman ETA when a bus is present, live phase, remaining
time, POG, pressures), commits at integer-second resolution, and advances.  This section
therefore defines (i) the online guard set shared by all scored arms,
(ii) the shared selection layer, and
(iii) the per-family candidate generators (\cref{tab:methods} maps all
fifteen scored arms).  Online
guards, the POG tracker, the bus-detection triggers, and the conditional-priority
rule layer R1--R11 live only here. They never constrain the exact program: the
program of \cref{sec:milp} contains only what a signal setting can change,
and every mechanism that \emph{observes} the corridor --- detection, cooldown,
spillback, congestion, veto, Purdue coordination, and the rule layer
(Sec.~\ref{sec:rule_tsp}) --- is part of the solution algorithm, not of the
program it solves. The two therefore differ in kind, not in degree: the program
has no notion of \emph{right now}.
All online quantities are integers (seconds, steps, vehicles, pax\,s).
