\textbf{State.} Per intersection: current phase, elapsed time, Kalman-filtered
main and side flow rates ($\alpha = 0.3$ exponential smoothing), main-approach
queue length, count of approaching buses within 120\,s ETA window, and minimum
bus ETA via Kalman tracker.

\textbf{Decision logic.} At each tick each junction applies the per-second rule set of Alg.~\ref{alg:centralised} to its Kalman-filtered state ($\approx$20 candidates per junction; Kalman smoothing $\alpha = 0.3$ for the HOLD/TRANSITION flow comparison). The full rule cascade--HOLD/TRANSITION thresholds, two-step phase rotation, and the benefit-vs-cost comparisons--is stated executable-form in Alg.~\ref{alg:centralised}; the scored run then commits through Alg.~\ref{alg:master} lines 6--9.

% ----------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Method I: Tabular Q-Learning (CPD-QL)}
\label{sec:sol_cpd}
% ----------------------------------------------------

CPD-QL learns action values $Q(s,a)$ for the single-event subproblem from
repeated experience.  The state $s$ is a discrete bin vector: headway deviation
(late/on-time/early), bus ETA to stop-line ($<$10 / [10,30) / $\ge$30\,s),
estimated delay (zero/low/high), and phase match (Boolean)--yielding
$3\times3\times3\times2 = 54$ states.

\noindent\textbf{Solves:} the single-event Q-learning problem: learn action
values $Q(s,a)$ maximising the headway-plus-delay reward below, then act
$\varepsilon$-greedily.  Nothing is optimised online beyond the argmax.

\paragraph{Objective mapping (Type~E, not Type~A).} CPD-QL proposes and commits
by $\arg\max_a Q(s,a)$ (Alg.~\ref{alg:cpdql}) with only the cost veto
$\theta{=}3.0$ and no $Z_1$ layer; its reward
Eq.~\eqref{eq:cpd_reward} is a genuine $Z_1{+}Z_3$ blend, so lateness is
optimised jointly with delay (Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives}, learned-commit column).

For action $a$, the reward is:
\begin{equation}
  r(a) = w_h\,\rho_{\text{bus}}\bigl(|\sigma_{\text{in}}| - |\sigma_{\text{out}}(a)|\bigr)
       + (1-w_h)\bigl((d_b^{\text{NA}} - d_b^{a})\,n_b - \Delta_{\text{car}}(a)\bigr)
  \label{eq:cpd_reward}
\end{equation}
with $w_h = 0.5$ (balanced headway/delay weighting).  Both terms are now in
the same currency, pax\,s: the headway term carries bus occupancy
$\rho_{\text{bus}}$ (matching the online Act step in
Alg.~\ref{alg:cpdql} and the deployed reward, where
$r = w_h\rho_{\text{bus}}(|\sigma_{\text{in}}|-|\sigma_{\text{out}}|) +
(1-w_h)(\text{bps}-\text{cpc})$), and the delay term carries the bus load
$n_b$ on the saved seconds minus the car passenger-seconds
$\Delta_{\text{car}}$.  A mixed-seconds reward would let $w_h$ blend
incommensurate units; the occupancy-scaled form keeps the objective a true
$Z_1{+}Z_3$ blend.
The Q-table is initialised from estimated immediate rewards
and updated via standard Q-learning with $\alpha_Q=0.1$, $\gamma_Q=0.9$,
$\varepsilon=0.1$.  Action selection is $\varepsilon$-greedy over a
7-action set $\{\text{NA}, \text{GE}_{\delta}: \delta \in
\{5,10,15\}\} \cup \{\text{INS}_{\delta}: \delta \in \{10,15,20\}\}$ --
an integer-second candidate set, deliberately sparse here (Method I is the
low-resolution baseline); the lattice-solved methods (deficit/DE/exact)
size the same tokens at any integer in the admissible band.

\noindent\textbf{Limitation:} CPD-QL has no hard pre-commit veto.  The
$\Delta_{\text{car}}$ term in Eq.\,\ref{eq:cpd_reward} penalises
net-negative actions in the reward, so the agent \emph{learns} to avoid
them, but nothing \emph{blocks} a harmful commit: under $\varepsilon$
exploration, and whenever the Q-values are still wrong early in training,
the $\arg\max$ can select an action whose true passenger cost exceeds its
bus benefit.  Other traffic is still priced in the reward, but only
softly, through learning rather than a gate.  This manifests as the
$+2.3\%$ total-delay increase scored in \cref{sec:res_overall}.

