\subsection{Method II: Harmony Search with LWR, (HSLWR)}
\label{sec:sol_wavegate}
% ----------------------------------------------------

WaveGate learns single-event durations with Harmony Search (HS)--a population-based
metaheuristic over a harmony memory of the $H$ best $(s,\delta)$ pairs--seeded by
a closed-form LWR shockwave duration and gated by a passenger cost-benefit test.
The full improvise--evaluate--update loop is stated executable-form in
Alg.~\ref{alg:wavegate}; what matters for the paper is the LWR seed and gate.

\noindent\textbf{Solves:} the integer duration $\delta$ minimising predicted
net passenger cost, searched by Harmony Search from the closed-form LWR seed
and admitted only through the main-vs-side gate below.

\paragraph{Objective mapping (Type~A).} WaveGate proposes by Harmony Search on
the LWR cost $J(\delta)$ and commits through the $Z_1$ layer: grant iff
\cref{eq:wavegate_balance} holds, then $\arg\min\Delta_{\text{net}}$ s.t.\
the $Z_4$ veto (Alg.~\ref{alg:wavegate} via Alg.~A0); the cleanest Type~A
example (Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives}, $Z_1$-net column).

\paragraph{LWR shockwave gate.}
WaveGate is a shockwave-informed, benefit-gated optimisation method that grants transit signal priority only when LWR-estimated queue clearance yields a net passenger benefit.
The bus approach queue is modelled via the LWR back-shockwave:
\begin{equation}
  w = -\frac{q_{\text{arr}}}{k_j - k_{\text{arr}}},
  \quad
  \delta^* = \max\!\left(0,\; \frac{n_0}{q_{\text{sat}} - q_{\text{arr}}} - d_b^{\text{NA}}\right)
  \label{eq:lwr}
\end{equation}
clamped to $[\delta_{\min},\delta_{\max}]$.  Read it as a deficit: the queue
of $n_0$ vehicles ahead of the bus needs $n_0/(q_{\text{sat}} - q_{\text{arr}})$
seconds to discharge at net saturation rate (both in veh and veh/s, so the
ratio is seconds), while the bus already absorbs $d_b^{\text{NA}}$ seconds of
that wait under no action; the extension covers the remainder, floored at
zero.  The cost-benefit gate is evaluated per intersection $i$, main section
against side section:
\begin{equation}
  \text{Grant}_i \iff \underbrace{(d_{b,i}^{\text{NA}} - d_{b,i}^{\delta})\,n_b \cdot \rho_{\text{bus}} - \Delta^{\text{main}}_{\text{car},i}(\delta)}_{\text{Main-section net (pax\,s)}}
  \;>\; \underbrace{\Delta^{\text{side}}_{\text{car},i}(\delta)}_{\text{Side-section cost (pax\,s)}}
  \label{eq:wavegate_balance}
\end{equation}
with $\rho_{\text{bus}}=40$, $\rho_{\text{car}}=1.5$ folded into the
$\Delta$ terms so both sides are in pax\,s.
$\Delta^{\text{main}}_{\text{car},i}$ is the car passenger-second change on
the bus's own (main) approach, negative when the platoon benefits, and
$\Delta^{\text{side}}_{\text{car},i}$ is the added car passenger-seconds on
the held cross (side) approach.

The gate compares the main-section net passenger effect against the
side-section cost at the acting intersection and grants TSP only when the
net exceeds the cost by the calibrated warrant.  Other traffic is therefore
priced explicitly on both approaches, not ignored.  This prevents the
net-harmful over-triggering observed in
CPD-QL (\cref{sec:res_overall}).

\subsubsection{Shockwave Profile Reconstruction from Simulation Data}
\label{sec:shockwave_profile}

The LWR shockwave gate (Eq.\,\ref{eq:lwr}) requires four shockwave speeds
derived from the flow--density fundamental diagram.  Following
\citet{AnsariEsfeh2025TSPCV}
(Fig.\,3 therein), we define the four shockwave speeds at intersection $i$:
\begin{align}
  v_{q,i} &= -\frac{q_{\mathrm{in},i}}{k_{j,i} - k_{f,i}}
    \label{eq:vq} \\[4pt]
  v_{c,i} &= \phantom{-}\frac{q_{s,i}}{k_{j,i} - k_{c,i}}
    \label{eq:vc} \\[4pt]
  v_{p,i} &= \phantom{-}\frac{q_{s,i} - q_{\mathrm{in},i}}{k_{c,i} - k_{f,i}}
    \label{eq:vp} \\[4pt]
  v_{d,i} &= v_{f,i}
    \label{eq:vd}
\end{align}
where $q_{\mathrm{in},i}$ is the measured approach flow (veh/s),
$k_{j,i}$ is jam density (veh/m), $k_{f,i} = q_{\mathrm{in},i}/v_{f,i}$
is the free-flow density at input flow, $k_{c,i} = q_{s,i}/v_{f,i}$ is
capacity density, $q_{s,i}$ is saturation flow, and $v_{f,i}$ is free-flow
speed.  Physically:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item $v_{q,i} < 0$: backward-forming shock at the upstream edge of the
        stopped queue (queuing shockwave, negative = propagates upstream);
  \item $v_{c,i} > 0$: forward-moving shock as the queue discharges during
        green (discharging shockwave);
  \item $v_{p,i}$: speed of the density transition between the dense queue
        and the arriving free-flow stream (pressure shockwave);
  \item $v_{d,i} = v_{f,i}$: free-flow speed at which vehicles depart
        once the queue has cleared (departing shockwave).
\end{itemize}



