\paragraph{Action families, not literals.} The action set is the three
current-phase families
\begin{equation}
  \Acal_i = \{\textsc{Hold}\;(\text{NA}),\; \textsc{Extend}\;(\text{GE}),\; \textsc{End-early}\;(\text{ER})\},
  \label{eq:cq2_actions}
\end{equation}
so the learned table is only $|\Scal_i|\times|\Acal_i| = 1458 \times 3 = 4374$
dense cells per junction -- against $4374^{|\Isp|}$ for a joint learner, the
per-junction independence is what keeps the table tractable, and the value that
the sparse v1 space wasted on rarely-fired insertion letters is spent on states
that actually recur.  A chosen \textsc{Extend}/\textsc{End-early} is a
\emph{family selector}; its exact magnitude is solved on the CTM within the
duration grid $\mathcal{D}_{\text{GE}}$/$\mathcal{D}_{\text{ER}}$ bounded by the
running green (the family-selector convention already used for the duration
grids in Sec.~\ref{sec:holding}), so the coarse three-way table never
has to encode seconds.

\paragraph{Cell-transition dynamics and dense reward.} The transition
$P^{\text{CTM}}$ is one rollout of the corridor cell-transition model:
$\hat{X}_{t+} = F^{\text{CTM}}(\hat{X}_t, a)$ advances the CTM cells under the
candidate phase change (exogenous arrivals supply the stochasticity), i.e.\
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:ctm_send}--\eqref{eq:ctm_update} evaluated one step ahead; the
delay learners' reward Eq.~\eqref{eq:cellq_reward} is its flattened one-stage
form.  The immediate reward is the CTM-propagated
\emph{corridor passenger surplus} of the change,
\begin{equation}
  R^{\text{c}}(s_{i,t}, a) \;=\; \underbrace{\Pi^{\text{thru}}_{i,t}(a)}_{\text{bus\,+\,through-mainline pax\,s saved}} \;-\; \underbrace{c^{\text{corr}}_{i,t}(a)}_{\text{measured cross + downstream landing cost}},
  \label{eq:cq2_reward}
\end{equation}
where $\Pi^{\text{thru}}$ is the transit-plus-through payoff of the phase
(the $\_nash\_phase\_total\_bps$ of the continuous game,
Sec.~\ref{sec:sol_nash}: $\rho_{\text{bus}}$ on any bus plus $\rho_{\text{car}}$
on the through-mainline pax the phase discharges) and $c^{\text{corr}}$ is the
measured-cross-plus-projected-cascade cost of Sec.~\ref{sec:gate_cascade}
applied bus-free downstream.  Because $\Pi^{\text{thru}}$ scores
\emph{through traffic}, not only bus passengers, $R^{\text{c}}$ is nonzero for
every family in every state -- the reward, like the state, is dense.  Both terms
are $Z_1$ passenger-delay quantities (\cref{eq:Z1}), so
\cref{eq:cq2_reward} is a one-stage $Z_1$ surplus, consistent with the
delay-learner objective mapping of Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives}.

\paragraph{Q-learning with a heuristic prior.} The table is updated by standard
temporal-difference Q-learning on the realised successor state $s'_{i}$,
\begin{equation}
  Q_i(s,a) \;\leftarrow\; Q_i(s,a) + \alpha\Bigl[\,R^{\text{c}}(s,a) + \gamma \max_{a'\in\Acal_i} Q_i(s',a') - Q_i(s,a)\,\Bigr],
  \label{eq:cq2_qupdate}
\end{equation}
with learning rate $\alpha$, discount $\gamma$, and $\varepsilon$-greedy
exploration.  Cold-start zeros are eliminated by a \emph{heuristic-seeded
optimistic initialisation}: an unvisited state is seeded with the CTM one-step
surplus of each family,
\begin{equation}
  Q_i^{(0)}(s,a) \;=\; R^{\text{c}}(s,a) \qquad \forall a \in \Acal_i,
  \label{eq:cq2_init}
\end{equation}
so a table it has never seen already ranks the families as the greedy CTM
heuristic would.  This makes the two deployed stages the same estimator at
different temperatures: with learning disabled the controller is the
deterministic Stage-1 heuristic $a^\star_{i,t} = \arg\max_{a} R^{\text{c}}(s_{i,t},a)$
(pure CTM greed), and with learning enabled \cref{eq:cq2_qupdate} lets it
depart from the one-step optimum to capture temporal effects the greedy score
cannot see -- e.g.\ an extension that pays this cell but saturates a downstream
cell one cycle later.  Every committed \textsc{Extend}/\textsc{End-early} still
passes the recoverability and Purdue-progression gates R8--R9 at execution
(Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards}), so learning changes \emph{which} admissible
family is chosen, never the admissible set.

% ----------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Method V: Nash Bargaining with Harmony Search (NashHS)}
\label{sec:sol_nash}
% ----------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Corridor-Level Cooperative Bargaining Game}

NashGate formulates TSP as a multi-player cooperative bargaining game,
extending single-intersection Nash bargaining \citep{AnsariEsfeh2025TSPCV} to
the full corridor.  Each signal phase at every corridor intersection is an independent
bargaining player; the player set is:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{G} = \bigl\{(i,p) : i \in \mathcal{I},\; p \in \mathcal{P}_i\bigr\},
  \qquad |\mathcal{G}| = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{I}} |\mathcal{P}_i|
  \label{eq:nash_players}
\end{equation}
For the Kelvin Grove corridor with $|\mathcal{I}|=12$ intersections and a
typical four-phase plan, $|\mathcal{G}| \approx 48$ players.  The corridor
game is solved at each bus detection event: Harmony Search improvises
candidate action durations $\delta$, and the corridor Nash product selects
the one maximising joint utility across all phases simultaneously.

\noindent\textbf{Solves:} $\arg\max_\delta$ Nash product (or weighted-sum
variant) of all players' utilities: the fair joint bargain over a single
event, not minimum delay.  Fairness across phases is the objective; delay
reduction is a hoped-for side effect.

\paragraph{Objective mapping (Type~B for the product, Type~A for the weighted sum).} The two Nash variants share a \emph{proposer} (Harmony Search over $\Lambda_{\text{HS}}$, pre-filtered by the balance gate) but differ at \emph{commit} (Alg.~\ref{alg:nash}).
\texttt{DCTSP\_BARGAIN\_SPM} commits through the $Z_1$ layer ($\arg\min\Delta_{\text{net}}$ on the weighted-sum-ranked pool--hence Type~A in \cref{tab:methods}), while \texttt{NASH\_BARGAIN} commits by $\max$ product \cref{eq:nash_product} s.t.\ C1--C7 (Type~B)--never by $\arg\min\Delta_{\text{net}}$.  The net surplus $\sum_p u_{i,p}$ happens to equal the $Z_1$ change in pax\,s units, but the product form vetoes any action that harms one phase severely even when $Z_1$ improves, which is why Nash-product can be $+55.2\%$ on $Z_1$ while satisfying its own fairness objective.  $Z_4$ enters only via the cascade folded into cpc; $Z_3$ only via MIN\_GAIN.  Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives} places the product variant in the fair-bargain column.

\paragraph{Player utilities.}
For candidate action $\delta$, player $(i,p)$ receives utility:
\begin{equation}
  u_{i,p}(\delta) =
  \begin{cases}
    \rho_{\mathrm{bus}}\bigl[d_{i,p_b^i}^{\mathrm{NA}} - d_{i,p_b^i}^{\delta}\bigr]
      + \rho_{\mathrm{car}}\bigl[D_{i,p_b^i}^{\mathrm{NA}} - D_{i,p_b^i}^{\delta}\bigr]
      & p = p_b^i \;\text{(bus phase at }i\text{)} \\[4pt]
    -\rho_{\mathrm{car}}\bigl[D_{i,p}^{\delta} - D_{i,p}^{\mathrm{NA}}\bigr]
      & p \in \mathcal{P}_i^c \;\text{(cross-traffic phase at }i\text{)}
  \end{cases}
  \label{eq:nash_util}
\end{equation}
where $d_{i,p_b^i}^{\bullet}$ is the passenger-second \emph{bus} delay and
$D_{i,p}^{\bullet}$ the passenger-second delay of the \emph{general (through)
traffic} on phase $p$, each evaluated at intersection $i$ under NO\_ACTION and
under $\delta$; $\rho_{\mathrm{bus}}$ and $\rho_{\mathrm{car}}$ are the bus and
car occupancies.  Crucially the bus-phase player's payoff is the delay saved for
\emph{all traffic that phase serves}--the bus passengers \emph{and} the through
cars discharged by the same green--not the bus alone.  A green extension that
speeds the bus also clears the mainline queue behind it, and pricing only the bus
would under-value the phase and bias the game toward inaction.  The two players
are then symmetric in units: the bus phase is credited the total passenger-second
delay it \emph{removes}, the cross phase is charged the total passenger-second
delay it \emph{incurs}, both in pax\,s.  Consequently the net bargaining surplus
$\sum_p u_{i,p}$ is exactly the change in \emph{total} corridor passenger delay,
so selecting the action of maximum surplus is identical to minimising total
passenger delay--the objective the controller is asked to optimise.

\paragraph{Disagreement point.}
The disagreement point $\eta_{i,p}(t)$ is the status-quo payoff each player
insists on at minimum--the utility received under NO\_ACTION.  Following
\citep{AnsariEsfeh2025TSPCV} we define it as the negative queue spillback risk:
\begin{equation}
  \eta_{i,p}(t) = -\frac{\bar{Q}_{i,p}(t)}{L_{i,p}}
  \label{eq:nash_disagree}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{Q}_{i,p}(t)$ is mean queue occupancy on approach $p$ at
intersection $i$ and $L_{i,p}$ is its link length.  A phase near spillback
($\bar{Q}/L\!\to\!1$) has a strongly negative disagreement point and will
resist actions that increase its queue.

\paragraph{Online realisation of the disagreement point.}
The deployed controller evaluates the bargain on the estimated state
(Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards}), so it realises $\eta$ as a \emph{state-dependent
transit-side threat}: the minimum transit gain required to strike a bargain rises
with the measured cross saturation $\hat{s}^{\text{cross}}_{i}(t)$,
\begin{equation}
  d_{T,i}(t) = \kappa^{\text{thr}}\,\hat{s}^{\text{cross}}_{i}(t)\,\rho_{\text{bus}},
  \qquad
  \text{admit an action only if } \text{bps}_{i}(\delta) - d_{T,i}(t) > 0,
  \label{eq:nash_online_threat}
\end{equation}
with gain $\kappa^{\text{thr}}$ (\texttt{NASH\_THREAT\_CROSS\_GAIN}, on under
\texttt{NASH\_STATE\_THREAT}).  The transit factor in the Nash product is then the
gain \emph{over this threat}, $\text{bps}-d_{T,i}$, not over a bare zero, so the
bargain becomes conservative exactly as the cross street approaches spillback and
declines every action once it is saturated--the causal, measured counterpart of
the negative $\eta$ in \cref{eq:nash_disagree}.  This is the principled form of
what a fixed minimum-gain constant did only crudely: a state-independent floor
cannot tell a cheap preemption on an empty cross street from a ruinous one on a
jammed one, whereas $d_{T,i}$ scales with the harm.  Varying the threat with state
--rather than the powers with demand--keeps the powers as policy
(\cref{eq:nash_power}) and avoids double-counting occupancy.

\subsubsection{Bargaining Constraints}

The corridor Nash bargaining problem is subject to the following constraints,
adapted from \citet{AnsariEsfeh2025TSPCV}
(Eqs.\,27--35 therein) to the multi-intersection corridor setting.

\paragraph{C1 \textnormal{(Individual rationality)}.}
No player accepts a utility below its disagreement point:
\begin{equation}
  u_{i,p}(\delta) \;\ge\; \eta_{i,p}(t) \qquad \forall\,(i,p)\in\mathcal{G}
  \label{eq:c_rationality}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{C2 \textnormal{(Queue clearance at TSP intersection)}.}
Let $i^*$ denote the intersection where the bus is currently detected.
The modified green must be sufficient to clear the bus queue:
\begin{equation}
  T_{i^*}^A + \frac{d_{i^*}^A}{v_{a,i^*}} \;=\; (\delta_{i^*} + \gamma_{i^*})\,C_{i^*}
  \label{eq:c_clearance}
\end{equation}
where $T_{i^*}^A$ is bus arrival time, $d_{i^*}^A$ remaining approach
distance, $v_{a,i^*}$ approach speed, $\delta_{i^*}$ the TSP duration
fraction, $\gamma_{i^*}$ the baseline green fraction, and $C_{i^*}$ cycle
length.

\paragraph{C3 \textnormal{(Maximum cross-traffic queue)}.}
For each cross-traffic phase at $i^*$, the resulting queue must not exceed
available storage:
\begin{equation}
  \bar{R}_{i^*,p}^{\delta}
    = \frac{1}{m_{i^*,p}}\sum_{j}\bigl(v_{q,i^*}\,\delta_{i^*}\,C_{i^*}
      + R_{i^*,p}\bigr)
    \;\le\; \sigma_p\,L_{i^*,p}
  \qquad \forall\,p\in\mathcal{P}_{i^*}^c
  \label{eq:c_queue}
\end{equation}
where $v_{q,i^*}$ is the queuing shockwave speed, $m_{i^*,p}$ lane count,
$R_{i^*,p}$ current residual queue, and $\sigma_p\in(0,1]$ the maximum
queue-to-storage fraction.

\paragraph{C4 \textnormal{(Bus approach speed)}.}
TSP must not require the bus to operate outside safe speed bounds:
\begin{equation}
  0.8\,v_i \;\le\; v_{a,i} \;\le\; 1.2\,v_i \qquad \forall\,i\in\mathcal{I}
  \label{eq:c_speed}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{C5 \textnormal{(Green end time)}.}
The TSP green extension must terminate before the cross-traffic minimum
green requirement:
\begin{equation}
  t_{i^*,p_b^{i^*}}^{\mathrm{end}} + \delta_{i^*}
    \;\le\; C_{i^*} - \sum_{p\ne p_b^{i^*}} G_{i^*}^{\min}
  \label{eq:c_greenend}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{C6 \textnormal{(Minimum green preservation)}.}
After TSP redistributes green time, all phases retain their minimum:
\begin{equation}
  g_{i,p,t} \;\ge\; G_i^{\min} \qquad \forall\,(i,p)\in\mathcal{G}
  \label{eq:c_ming}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{C7 \textnormal{(TSP start and duration fractions)}.}
The TSP start fraction $\gamma_i^{\mathrm{tsp}}$ and duration fraction
$\delta_i$ are non-negative and sum to at most unity:
\begin{equation}
  \gamma_i^{\mathrm{tsp}} \ge 0, \quad
  \delta_i \ge 0, \quad
  \gamma_i^{\mathrm{tsp}} + \delta_i \le 1
  \qquad \forall\,i\in\mathcal{I}
  \label{eq:c_fractions}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Corridor Nash Product Objective}

NashGate selects the action duration $\delta^*$ maximising the generalised
Nash bargaining product across all corridor phases:
\begin{equation}
  \delta^* = \arg\max_{\delta \;\in\; \Lambda_{\mathrm{HS}}}
    \prod_{(i,p)\,\in\,\mathcal{G}}
    \Bigl[\,u_{i,p}(\delta) - \eta_{i,p}(t) + \varepsilon\,\Bigr]^{\alpha_{i,p}}
  \label{eq:nash_product}
\end{equation}
subject to C1--C7, where $\varepsilon > 0$ prevents zero-product
singularities and:
\begin{equation}
  \alpha_{i,p} =
  \begin{cases}
    \alpha & p = p_b^i \;\text{(bus-phase bargaining power)} \\
    \beta  & p \in \mathcal{P}_i^c \;\text{(cross-traffic bargaining power)}
  \end{cases}
  \label{eq:nash_power}
\end{equation}
The product form enforces strict multi-objective balance: an action that
imposes severe delay on any single phase $(i,p)$--at any corridor
intersection--drives that factor toward zero, suppressing the entire product.
This is strictly stronger than the two-player form: even a large bus benefit
at $i^*$ cannot compensate a severely harmed cross-traffic phase at a
downstream intersection $i \ne i^*$.

\paragraph{Bargaining power is a policy parameter, not a tuning knob.}
The powers $(\alpha,\beta)=(p_T,p_C)$ are the one place in the program where a
value judgement enters.  Holding the passenger counts fixed in the payoffs
$u_{i,p}$, they set how much an agency values a transit passenger-second over a
general-traffic one.  Demand is already \emph{inside} the payoffs--a fuller bus
earns a larger $u_{i,p}$ through its occupancy, before any power is applied--so
weighting by demand would double-count it; and signal timing is already inside
the disagreement point $\eta_{i,p}$ of \cref{eq:nash_disagree}, since a longer
cross green is a costlier status quo.  The powers must therefore encode something
\emph{outside} the payoffs to be meaningful.  We accordingly report two
principled settings rather than a fitted value: the symmetric bargain
$(p_T,p_C)=(1,1)$, which values a transit and a general-traffic passenger-second
equally and is the genuine Nash solution; and the utilitarian limit
$(p_T,p_C)=(0,1)$, at which the product collapses to the plain net surplus
$\sum_p u_{i,p}$ and the controller minimises total passenger delay (the setting
reported as the delay-optimal arm).  A premium $p_T>1$ that favours transit
beyond its headcount is a legitimate policy, but one that must be declared as
such rather than fitted.

\paragraph{Candidate generation.} The candidate set $\Lambda_{\mathrm{HS}}$ is generated by the Harmony Search of Alg.~\ref{alg:wavegate} and pre-filtered by the balance gate (Eq.\,\ref{eq:wavegate_balance}) before Nash evaluation, mirroring the two-stage filter of \citep{AnsariEsfeh2025TSPCV}.  Both Nash variants are stated executable-form in Alg.~\ref{alg:nash}.

% ----------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Green Reallocation (GR) -- Zero-Cycle-Cost TSP}
\label{sec:sol_gr}
% ----------------------------------------------------

Green Reallocation (GR) is a TSP action type that redistributes green time
\emph{within} an existing cycle rather than adding net cycle duration.
Unlike Green Extension (GE), which lengthens the bus-priority phase beyond its
nominal allocation, GR achieves bus-priority by \emph{compressing} one or more
cross-traffic phases and transferring that time to the bus phase.  The cycle
length $C_i$ is unchanged; only the intra-cycle phase split is modified.

\paragraph{Decision rule.}
Let $g_{i,p_b}^0$ be the nominal bus-phase green, $g_{i,p}^0$ the nominal
cross-traffic green, and $\Delta$ the requested reallocation window.  GR is
feasible at intersection $i$ iff:
\begin{equation}
  g_{i,p}^0 - \Delta \ge G_i^{\min} \quad \forall\; p \in \mathcal{P}_i^c
  \label{eq:gr_feas}
\end{equation}
If feasible, the action sets $g_{i,p_b} = g_{i,p_b}^0 + \Delta$ and
$g_{i,p} = g_{i,p}^0 - \Delta / |\mathcal{P}_i^c|$ for each cross-traffic
phase, preserving $\sum_p g_{i,p} = C_i - L_i$.

\paragraph{Reward and use.} GR enters the BARGAIN reward identically to GE/PI (net weighted pax\,s change); with no net cycle time added it typically saves less bus delay at lower cross-traffic cost, so it passes the balance gate more easily.  $\Delta$ is chosen by the DE optimiser; GR is committed iff it beats GE, PI, and NO\_ACTION (see Alg.~\ref{alg:nash}--\ref{alg:mp}).

\paragraph{Outcome in batch results.}
GR accounts for the GR column of \cref{tab:kg_tsp} (e.g.\ 3 GR/run for the champion): a small share of detection events with $\sim$8--12\,s windows, most effective when the bus arrives near the end of a long red after cross-traffic has discharged.

% ----------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Method VI: MARL Heuristic and Method VII: Max-Pressure (no $Z_1$ commit)}
\label{sec:sol_marl_mp}
% ----------------------------------------------------

Two scored families have no solution subsection above because they never touch
the $Z_1$ layer at all; they are included here so Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives} is
complete.  Full step-by-step versions are Algs.~\ref{alg:marl}--\ref{alg:mp}.

\paragraph{MARL heuristic (\texttt{DCTSP\_MARL}, Type~A with corridor coupling).}
\emph{Proposes} $\text{local}(a)=w_A(\text{bus}+\text{main})-w_C\,\text{cross}$
per candidate and \emph{commits} $\arg\max_a \text{global}(a)$ with
$\text{global}(a)=\text{local}(a)-\text{chain-cost}(a)$ over a 3-hop downstream
chain (Alg.~\ref{alg:marl}), then through the $Z_4$ veto.  In $Z$-terms this is
Type~A with the $Z_2$/cascade cost made additive rather than gated: $Z_1$
minimised including mainline savings, $Z_4$ capped, $Z_3$ only via congestion
gate.  It is the best-bus arm (13.3\,s/pax) precisely because the chain prices
corridor delay the local $Z_1$ commit misses.

\paragraph{Max-pressure FIX/FLEX/LB (Type~C, stability--not delay).}
\emph{Proposes and commits} by $\arg\max_p\sum_{m\in p}\max(\pi_m,0)$ with
pax-weighted pressures ($\text{bus}{\times}40$, $\text{car}{\times}1.5$;
Sec.~\ref{sec:maxpressure}, Alg.~\ref{alg:mp}).  FIX splits green per cycle,
FLEX re-evaluates per $T_A$, LB adds dwelling-bus blockage scaling and a
downstream-saturation discount.  In $Z$-terms it maximises stability
(throughput, $Z_4$-like) and never minimises $Z_1$ or $Z_3$; the $Z_4$ veto and
congestion gate are the only exact-program quantities it respects.  Hence it can
improve bus delay ($-15\%$) while harming $Z_1$ ($+6$--$8\%$) without
contradiction--it solves a different problem.




