\section{Discussion}

Three findings emerge from the Kelvin Grove corridor experiments:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{On a well-timed, car-dominated corridor, per-junction transit
priority is hard to make net-beneficial.} Of fifteen strategies, only the gated
CellQLearn (\texttt{CELLQLEARN\_GATED}) achieves the best five-seed mean total-delay
reduction ($-5.5\%$), and several otherwise-reasonable controllers (Centralised,
Nash, CTM grid search) gridlock the corridor by $+31$ to $+58\%$. The bottleneck
is not the search strategy but the \emph{cost model}: a decision that prices only
the local cross approach cannot see the downstream gridlock its released platoon
will cause.
\item \textbf{Pricing the downstream network cost is what turns priority from a
lottery into a win.} The difference between the un-gated CellQLearn ($+4.7\%$)
and the gated variant ($-5.5\%$) is entirely the total-passenger net-benefit gate
fed by the measured cross cost and the projected-saturation cascade
(\cref{sec:gate_cascade}). The gate does not reduce the action \emph{rate}
so much as move actions away from the demand realisations where they cascade.
\item \textbf{Bus-favourable and corridor-favourable are distinct objectives.}
The MARL heuristic and the passenger-weighted max-pressure controllers deliver
the largest bus savings ($-15$ to $-20\%$) but load the cross street enough to be
net-neutral-to-negative on total passenger delay. A corridor operator choosing a
controller must decide which of the two it is optimising; no single arm here is
best on both.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Why a full MILP if one gate explains the swing? Which parts are
necessary?} The reviewer question is fair: the gated-versus-unguarded
CellQLearn delta ($+4.7\%$ to $-5.5\%$) is the largest single effect in
\cref{tab:kg_results}, yet the paper develops signal switching, queues,
offsets, bus clocks, and the rules the online layer applies (R1--R11,
\cref{sec:rule_tsp}).  The answer is that the gate is only
\emph{sufficient} because the rest of the program prices its two sides
correctly.  Concretely:
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.9cm}p{2.7cm}p{10.9cm}@{}}
\hline
MILP block & Online counterpart & Necessity evidence in this paper \\
\hline
Queue/discharge + queue-waiting sums (\cref{eq:gate_discharge,eq:gate_queue}, \ref{eq:mv_q}--\ref{eq:mv_waitsum}) & Measured-side floor + CTM/LWR benefit estimate & Without it bus benefit vs.\ car cost are incommensurable; arms that misprice either side (Centralised $+58\%$, CTMGS $+49\%$) gridlock. \\
Bus clocks + schedule caps (\cref{eq:arr_prop,eq:sched_cap}, $Z_3$) & ETA window, $\hat{\sigma}^+$, takeover, MIN\_GAIN & Removes grants to on-time buses (conditional-priority principle); unguarded arms grant where no lateness exists. \\
Offset/progression + bus coupling (\cref{eq:offset_state,eq:prog_bus}) & Projected-saturation cascade (Sec.~\ref{sec:gate_cascade}) & The cascade \emph{is} the online projection of R4/progression: un-gated CellQLearn acts at the same rate but where downstream cost is high (Tables~\ref{tab:kg_results}--\ref{tab:kg_tsp}). \\
R1 balance (\cref{eq:rule_balance}) & Net-benefit gate (\cref{eq:wavegate_balance}, \ref{eq:net_pick}) & The 10.2-point gated swing; also the difference between MARL ($-1.7\%$) and Nash-product ($+55\%$). \\
R2--R3/R5--R7 (phase, cooldown, horizon, legality) & $\hat{\Ucal}$ (\cref{eq:online_det,eq:online_phase}), A0 & Prevents wrong-phase GE, oscillation, and illegal splits; no arm succeeds with them disabled. \\
$Z_4$ cap (\cref{eq:Z4}) & $Z_4$ gate in A0/selection layer & Shared veto (300\,veh\,s) that discards corridor-harmful survivors before the scalar $\arg\min$. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
In short: the gate decides, but the MILP defines \emph{what the gate
compares}.  Dropping the physical system (queues, clocks, offsets) leaves the gate
with nothing to price; dropping R1 leaves an accurate price unused.  The
minimal deployable subset this evaluation supports is therefore:
(i) a bus-benefit estimator with schedule conditioning, (ii) a measured
cross-cost plus a downstream-cascade term, and (iii) a hard net-benefit gate
with phase/cooldown/spillback feasibility--exactly R1+R4 plus clocks and
queues.  Phase-rotation, variable-phase skipping, and the full Nash product
are not necessary on Kelvin Grove (they never outperform the gated learner
here) and are retained as candidate generators only.

\paragraph{Statistical limits and fair calibration (read before citing a number).}
All headline numbers are means over five eval seeds (300--700); no confidence
intervals, standard deviations, or significance tests are reported, so no
claim of statistical significance is made--``best'' throughout means best
five-seed mean, and the demand-sensitivity sweep (Phase~3) is the only
robustness check beyond those seeds. Calibration was held fixed and fair
across arms: identical state feed, identical guard protocol A0, identical eval
seeds, per-arm veto/gain parameters disclosed in \cref{tab:methods}.
The catastrophic arms ($+31$ to $+58\%$) received the same calibration budget;
their failure is the finding, not a setup artefact--\cref{tab:kg_tsp}
shows they do not act more often than the champion, they act where the
downstream cost is high, which is exactly what a missing cascade term predicts.

\section{Conclusions and Recommendations}
\label{sec:conclusions}

This paper presented a proof-of-concept clairvoyant MILP formulation for corridor-level TSP (never solved) and a family of online heuristics evaluated over five random-seed replications on the 12-intersection Kelvin Grove Corridor only:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{The gated CellQLearn has the best five-seed mean} on the Kelvin
Grove Corridor ($-5.5\%$ total, $-8.4\%$ bus, $-4.8\%$ car, five-seed mean; no significance test claimed). It
pairs CTM Q-learning with a total-passenger net-benefit gate driven by a measured
cross cost and a projected-saturation downstream cascade cost
(\cref{sec:gate_cascade}).
\item \textbf{The network cost model, not the search strategy, is decisive.} The
un-gated CellQLearn is $+4.7\%$ and several strong controllers (Centralised
$+58\%$, Nash-product $+55\%$, CTM grid search $+49\%$) gridlock the corridor,
because a decision priced on the local cross approach alone cannot see the
downstream gridlock its released platoon causes. Adding the gate and cascade
converts the same learner from net-harmful to net-beneficial.
\item \textbf{Passenger-weighted max-pressure and the MARL heuristic are
bus-favourable but corridor-neutral.} They deliver the largest bus savings
($-15$ to $-20\%$) but load the cross street enough to be net-neutral-to-negative
on total passenger delay; the MP-TSP-LB lane-blockage terms narrow but do not
close this gap.
\item \textbf{The benefit is demand-sensitive.} A six-scalar demand sweep of the
champion shows the gain is concentrated near the nominal operating point and does
not robustly generalise to lighter or heavier demand -- the residual mismatch
between the priced cost and the true network response remains an open problem.
\item \textbf{The exact MILP provides a normative formulation, not a solved benchmark} (Sec.~\ref{sec:milp_status}, Theorem~\ref{thm:milp_bellman}) and a common
framework for comparing statistical solution methods across network geometries
and demand levels.
\end{enumerate}

Future work: extending the downstream cascade beyond a single route and pricing
multi-approach spillback so the benefit generalises across demand; completing the
Logan Road corridor evaluation (\cref{sec:logan}); and joint $Z_1$/$Z_4$
optimisation.

\end{linenumbers}
\end{doublespacing}

