\section{Appendix: Solution-Algorithm Pseudocode}
\label{sec:appendix_pseudo}
% ==============================================================================

This appendix is the executable companion to
\cref{sec:solutions}--\ref{sec:selection_layer} and
\cref{tab:methods}.  \cref{sec:milp} defines the whole-horizon,
clairvoyant, integer-time plant benchmark, which the code never solves
jointly.  What the code \emph{does} at each decision epoch
$t\in\Tsp^{\text{trig}}$ (bus detection, timer tick, or empty-phase event) is
exactly the procedures below: build an estimated state $\hat{X}_t$, enforce
the online guards of \cref{sec:online_guards}, generate one family's
candidates, then commit with that family's rule--Type~A $Z_1$-net
(\cref{eq:net_pick}), Type~E Q-argmax, Type~B bargain, Type~C pressure, or
Type~D rules (Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives}).  Only Type~A passes through the
shared $Z_1$ selection layer; the others commit on their own objectives.
Reading Algorithm~A0 first answers the paper's central operational question:
what a typical corridor should do, step by step, under each objective it might
actually pursue (least delay, fairness, stability, or rules).

Notation shared by all algorithms (all integers: seconds, vehicles, pax\,s) is fixed in \cref{tab:alg_notation}; algorithms use it without redefinition.  Learners train on seeds 800--1100 ($\varepsilon = 0.30$) then freeze for scoring on seeds 300--700.

