\paragraph{How to read it.} Physical system first (signal switching $\to$ flow $\to$
forcing $\to$ legality), then counting (queue-waiting sums $\to$ $Z_1$), then time
coupling (offsets $\to$ progression $\to$ bus clocks), finally the criterion.
Everything above is a signal setting or a consequence of one. The mechanisms
that \emph{observe} the corridor --- trigger epochs
(\cref{eq:bus_present,eq:trig_source}), the Purdue POG state and loss
(\cref{eq:pog_ewma,eq:pog_loss_zero}), and the policy layer R1--R11
(\cref{eq:rule_balance,eq:rule_headway}) --- are \emph{not} rows of
this program; they are the online layer of
\cref{sec:online_guards}. Deployed code (\cref{sec:appendix_pseudo}): one greedy event
at a time from estimated state $\hat{X}_t$ under the real-time checks $\hat{\Ucal}$.

\paragraph{Worked miniature example (space--time).}
\cref{fig:spacetime} runs the whole program on three junctions over
180\,s: cycle $C = 60$, bus green 20\,s, integer steps throughout.  Read each
element as a program variable.  The green blocks are the operations
$x_{i,p,t} = 1$; their stagger (0/25/55\,s) is the offset schedule
$\phi_{i,t}$.  Circles are the timetable $\bar{\tau}_{b,i}$ (5/30/55\,s);
the red path is the chosen bus clock $\tau_{b,i}$ (5/30--48/73\,s), with the
assignment $e_{b,i,t}$ picking one step per row.  At J2 a standing queue
($Q > 0$) holds the bus 18\,s (horizontal red segment: time passes, position
does not); the event $y_{\text{GE}} = 1$, $\delta = 10$ extends the green to
55\,s so the bus departs at 48\,s instead of waiting for the next cycle at
