\section{Appendix: Solver Viability and Reductions}
\label{sec:appendix_viability}
% ==============================================================================

This appendix collects the solver mechanics---what makes the exact program
tractable and what a solver claim would have to cover---which are not part of
the program itself.

\paragraph{Solver viability, stated before any number is quoted.}
The viability question is settled by the variable count, and the count is
governed by $\Tsp$, not by $|\Isp|$. At $\Delta t=1$\,s over the full scored
horizon the parent program carries the $\sim10^6$-constraint scale of
Sec.~\ref{sec:milp_status} and is out of reach. This subprogram is a different
object and should not inherit that verdict unexamined. In the order in which
the reductions bite:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Let the settings, not the gate, be the decision}
(\cref{eq:set_window}). This is the dominant term, but it is worth being
precise about \emph{why}, because the obvious description of it is wrong. The
window indicators $z$ do \emph{not} disappear: there are still of order
$|\Isp|\,|\Psp_i|\,|\Tsp|$ of them, and at $|\Isp|=12$, $|\Psp_i|=4$,
$|\Tsp|=400$ that is $\sim19{,}200$ binaries either way. What changes is that
they are \emph{forced} rather than \emph{free}. The solver no longer searches a
gate field over an exponential space of open/close patterns with the settings
as a by-product; it searches $3|\Isp| + \sum_i|\Psp_i|$ integers and the gate
follows. That is the difference between a program whose search space grows
exponentially in $|\Tsp|$ and one that does not, and it is what makes any
solver claim conceivable here at all.
\item \emph{Compress the time axis to events.} \emph{This} is what moves the
variable count. In a retiming horizon the only steps that matter are phase
boundaries: at most $2|\Psp_i|$ per cycle per junction, so a $400$-step grid
compresses to roughly $50$--$80$. Carrying the queue recursion on that coarser
grid (or on a liquid rather than integer occupancy) takes the leading term
from $\sim19{,}200$ to $\sim10^3$.
\item \emph{Shorten the horizon to a receding window.} Re-solve over
$1$--$4$ cycles and advance. The layer is a retiming layer, so a horizon
longer than a few cycles buys nothing and costs everything.
\item \emph{Aggregate cars by approach.} \cref{eq:cons_link,eq:cons_phase}
already avoid lane and movement granularity on the car side; do not reintroduce
it for buses either---one tracked entity per timetable bus, not one per
occupant.
\item \emph{Warm start.} Seed $(\hat{O},\hat{G},\hat{C})$ at the incumbent plan
and the queue at the measured state. Retiming problems are small perturbations
of a feasible point, and the incumbent supplies one.
\end{enumerate}

With (1)--(5) the subprogram is a mixed-integer program of order
$10^3$--$10^4$ binaries and a horizon of a few hundred steps, which is routine
for a modern MILP solver (Gurobi, or CP-SAT where the window indicators of
\cref{eq:set_window} dominate) and admits a \emph{reported} optimality gap.
Restricting further---fixing $\hat{G}$ and $\hat{C}$ and solving for
$\hat{O}$ alone---leaves a program small enough to solve to proven optimality
and is the natural first target, since it is also the only tier that matches the
offsets-only evidence discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec:offset_recourse}. We make no
claim that any tier has been solved; \cref{sec:milp_status} stands, and
this appendix narrows what a solver claim would have to cover rather than
making one.

